\par\par \b \uc2  ECB Watch: ECB Seen Hands Off On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market As Fin Mins Meet \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikako Mogi \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2000\par 13:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT -(Dow Jones)- The Euro looks set to test new lows, but the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably do nothing more than grin and bear it, maintaining its hands-off policy on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if pressure from euro-zone governments begins to swell. \par \par \uc2  Speculation resurfaced overnight of a possible first-ever ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as euro zone finance ministers and ECB officials prepare for a prescheduled meeting this weekend in France. \par \par \uc2  Citing unnamed delegation sources, the Financial Times Deutschland reported Thursday that the Finance Ministers agenda includes a discussion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market measures - including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to prop up the euro. \par \par \uc2  According to the Maastricht Treaty, Finance Ministers can make recommendations to the ECB on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The extent of the ministers' power has been the subject of controversy, however, as it calls into question the independence of the ECB. The ECB can ultimately reject any calls for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ministers. \par \par \uc2  With euro-11 Finance Ministers meeting Friday and the euro falling below $0.87 Wednesday, the ECB is in a tight squeeze. \par \par \uc2  The French, who currently hold the E.U. presidency, have recently made loud calls for the ministers to take a more active role in financial policy - which some have seen as a threat to the ECB's independence. France had only several years of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence before joining the euro and was notoriously \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange rate policy. \par \par \uc2  Now it seems the ECB's credibility and independence may be called into question, if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves the impression that finance ministers forced the ECB to take action. \par \par \uc2  Market participants say the ECB should stay out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because there's little it can do, particularly against the bear market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  "The ECB is even more than before loathe to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Jeremy Hawkins, chief economist at Bank of America. An \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't work in the first place against the market tide but it would be even less effective now that the damage has been done by a politician earlier this week, he says. \par \par \uc2  The euro's latest plight is partially seen triggered by comments on Monday by German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, who said the euro's exchange rate should be more reason for joy than concern. Schroeder backpedalled on Wednesday, saying the recent euro trend isn't good. \par \par \uc2  Germany's and euro zone's economic power is so strong that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders will sooner or later have to react, Schroeder said. \par \par \uc2  Hawkins says the finance ministers have to say something at the weekend meeting to repair the damage done by Schroeder and forge a united front in support of the euro. \par \par \uc2  But will the ECB preempt that? \par \par \uc2  A spokesman for the ECB would only say that an unusual statement issued by ECB President Wim Duisenberg on May 5, a day after the euro plunged to new lows at that time, "continues to be valid." In the statement, Duisenberg said he understood public concerns about the euro's weakness and the ECB monitors the exchange rate very closely. He also sought to assure the public that "the future of the euro is that of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , based on price stability and the strength of the European economy." \par \par \uc2  "I don't believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is really an option for the ECB," says Uwe Angenedt, chief economist at BHF-Bank. "The ECB is very concerned about the weak euro. But the ECB can't do a lot" because the euro's 20-month downtrend is based on market perception that the weak euro could stall growth via higher inflation and widen growth differentials with the U.S. That puts further pressure on the euro to depreciate. "It's a vicious circle," Angenendt says. \par \par \uc2  The ECB is caught "between a rock and a hard place," says Adolf Rosenstock, head of European economics at Nomura International plc. The weak euro could trigger an inflation spiral, but aggressive tightening to counter that could raise fears of an overkill on the current economic upswing in the euro area. "It's a lose-lose situation" for the ECB, Rosenstock says. \par \par \uc2  ABN Amro's Luca Jellinek says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  always becomes a consideration when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets a new record. But the market is long in euros and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 's success ratio is small when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is treading lower. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB now is asking for trouble. It'll be a slugging match between the market and the ECB, and the market has got more money than the ECB," Jellink says. \par \par \uc2  Market participants say the best the ECB could do is to wait for the market to find its floor and ride on the back of a rebound. They say the weekend meeting could provide a cushion for the euro's downtrend. \par \par \uc2  With Japanese authorities expressing concerns about the euro's weakness against the yen, the Bank of Japan may be more willing to cooperate than the U.S. Federal Reserve, market participants say. And with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rich experience in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, increasing prospects of a coordinated action by the BOJ and the ECB could put a brake on the euro selling. \par \par \uc2  Market nervousness may intensify on such speculation and help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  find its bottom. As soon as market tide turns, that's when the market should more seriously contemplate ECB action, Hawkins says. \par \par \uc2  But the timing isn't seen ripe for the near term. \par \par \uc2  -By Chikako Mogi, Dow Jones Newswires; 49-69-29725-500; chikako.mogi@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1103 4270 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9704d80\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
