\par\par \b \uc2  Further Drop Expected After Euro Falls To New Low Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2000\par 10:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The euro's record-breaking falling streak against the yen isn't likely to end anytime soon, traders in Tokyo say. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a new record low of Y93.53 during the Tokyo lunch hour, briefly rebounded, then changed direction once more and fell to yet another series of new lows en route to a bottom of Y93.08 during the European morning. \par \par \uc2  The nose-dive in the euro versus the yen comes as sentiment toward the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in general has fallen into the gutter. Late Wednesday, the euro fell to an all-time low against the U.S. dollar as well, sliding to $0.8811. \par \par \uc2  But traders said that the euro's downside risks against the yen are especially pronounced. That's because Japanese investors bought large amounts of euro-denominated assets when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still strong, and at some point those investors will probably move to dump those euro assets even if they suffer big losses. Some speculators are building short euro positions amid speculation that Japanese investors' euro selling will accelerate soon, traders say. \par \par \uc2  "Institutional investors in Japan have simply given up the so-called 'Euro Dream' that they held in 1998," says Kenneth Landon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in Tokyo. The investors are "in the process of limiting their exposure to euro-denominated assets." \par \par \uc2  A slew of other factors is making players bearish about the euro. \par \par \uc2  One factor is the economy. Although economic growth in the Euro-zone has improved recently, it's still nowhere near the U.S.'s go-go economy, and that's causing money flows out of Europe. \par \par \uc2  Things are looking up in Japan, too, so investors are shifting their euro-denominated portfolios to the yen, said Yukihiko Hashimoto, senior vice president and head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Sanwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  Another factor is the apparent lack of concern among European authorities about the euro weakness, and the resulting lack of worries in the market about a euro-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, says that the only way to stop the euro's slide is for euro-zone authorities to assert their readiness and willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "The market doesn't take euro-zone authorities very seriously because of their apparent lack of a unified (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy," Nakahira, currently a vice-chairman of the Institute of International Economic Studies, told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    ECB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Not Out Of The Question\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders aren't dismissing the possibility of European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  completely. And if the yen shoots up too sharply against\par the euro, traders feel that Japanese authorities, not the Europeans,\par might take the initiative in supporting the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Perhaps hinting at things to come, Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the\par Japanese Finance Ministry's International Bureau, warned Wednesday that\par MOF is "carefully watching" movements in the euro/yen and the dollar/yen\par and added that the euro should be stronger considering economic\par fundamentals.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But even if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or the ECB actually did decide to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , many\par traders doubt the action would have anything but limited, short-term\par impact.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In fact, any large rebound in the euro/yen following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par would probably only provide investors looking to unload euro holdings\par with a chance to sell, traders say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sanwa's Hashimoto expects the euro to continue to drift lower for the\par rest of the year, with Y93.00 serving as an important support level. If\par that level is breached, however, he expects the euro to tumble as low as\par Y90.30.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A survey of major Japanese companies conducted by Nihon Keizai Shimbun\par showed that the corporate sector also feels that the euro has more room\par to fall against the yen. Companies said they expect the euro to move in\par a range between Y90 and Y110 during the half-year period between\par September and March 2001.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9604eeb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
