\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Euro Falls To New Low Vs Yen But Finds Bottom \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2000\par 07:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- It looked like the euro's inexorable fall against the yen would continue unabated Wednesday as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell in early Asian trading to a new all-time low against its Japanese counterpart. \par \par \uc2  But traders said an unlikely player helped boost, indirectly, the euro against the yen later in the day - the Reserve Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  At one point the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a new low of Y93.53, dragged down by selling by foreign funds and Japanese banks. But from there the euro shot back up a half a yen to about Y94.00, just around the same time that rumors that the RBA was selling U.S. dollars to support the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made the rounds. \par \par \uc2  The apparent RBA action caused the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise against the yen as well, triggering a drop in the yen against other cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The aussie-yen went up on the aussie \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that caused the yen to be sold against its cross \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Takeo Suzuki, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust & Banking. \par \par \uc2  But even though the euro came off its intraday low against the yen, it remained below its level in New York Tuesday as bearish sentiment toward the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained intact. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also lower against the dollar, while the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slightly higher against the yen in range-bound trading. \par \par \uc2  Traders are divided as to whether the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will post further losses against the yen and the dollar in the near future. \par \par \uc2  Suzuki said he thinks the euro's intraday low Wednesday will serve as a support level for the short term and that a rise to around Y94.50 is possible. \par \par \uc2  Others said, however, that euro bearishness is alive and well, and that a drop below Y90.00 isn't out of the question in the short term. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y105.83, slightly above Y105.82 late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at Y106.43 late Tuesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.8875, lower than $0.8903 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $0.8968 in Tokyo Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y93.93, down from Y94.19 in New York late Tuesday. The euro was at Y95.47 in Tokyo late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling was at $1.4557, up from $1.4545 in New York but lower than\par $1.4569 in Tokyo Tuesday.\par -0- 06/09/00 05-31G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders "can't get rid of expectations that the euro will continue to\par fall," said Ryohei Muramatsu, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Commerbank in\par Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It will be difficult (for the euro) to rise back above $0.9000 and I\par think the market will begin to search the euro's bottom," he said.\par "Since markets overshoot, you can't deny the possibility" that the euro\par will fall to Y90.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market's belief that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the euro anytime soon is one major factor that's weighing on the\par single European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kosuke Nakahira, a former Japanese vice finance minister for\par international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires that the only way for\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities to stop the euro's drop is to indicate\par that they are ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if needed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The euro's weakness is clearly not based on fundamentals and stems\par mainly from the fact that the market doesn't take euro-zone authorities\par very seriously because of their apparent lack of a unified policy," said\par Nakahira, who is currently the vice chairman of private research body\par Institute for International Economic Studies.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some players, however, don't dismiss the possibility of\par euro-supportive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  out of hand.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If (the euro) fell rapidly, I think there's a good chance that\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be conducted," Nomura Trust's Suzuki said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese officials, at least, hint that they would take action if the\par yen rises too sharply against the euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Zembei Mizoguchi, the head of the Finance Ministry's international\par bureau, said Wednesday that the euro "should strengthen based on\par economic fundamentals," adding that Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities are\par closely watching moves in the euro-yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But some traders said that mixed signals about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy coming\par out of Europe suggest that an ECB-led \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely anytime\par soon.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They pointed to German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder's remark Monday -\par that the weakness of the euro "should be more reason for joy than reason\par for concern" when exports are considered - as evidence that euro-zone\par officials aren't too worried about the home \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's collapse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Schroeder said he welcomes euro weakness and he is the head of state,\par after all," said Commerzbank's Muramatsu. "The ECB will probably only\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a very last resort."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the euro has moved sharply over the past few sessions, the\par dollar-yen has been fairly stable. Wednesday, the dollar stayed in a\par narrow range between Y105.62 and Y105.95. Traders said worries that the\par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell yen if the dollar fell below\par Y105.00 are making players reluctant to build up long yen positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Kenneth McCallum, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par ken.mccallum@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates which accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture which is updated\par hourly.\par \f28 \par \par 