\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Outlook: Slowing US Growth Should Weigh On Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2000\par 00:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Slowing U.S. economic growth is expected to weigh on the dollar in September, especially against the yen, but whether the euro will finally be able to take advantage of what should be good news is debatable. \par \par \uc2  "I think the way the euro's been trading recently is basically reflective of very bad sentiment," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. "It doesn't seem to get any support from good news." \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did seem to finally get a boost from bad news in the U.S. last Friday, gaining over a cent following the biggest drop in U.S. nonfarm payrolls in nine years. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the euro was at $0.8997, up sharply from $0.8878 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was buying Y105.87, much lower than Y106.68 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's rally followed new all-time lows earlier in the week of Y94 and $0.8837, and some traders think that the sell-off in the wake of last week's European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 25-basis-point rate hike was overdone. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's still struggling, but if you look at the fundamental background, it's never been as good as it is now," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank International in London, noting narrowing spreads in both short-term and long-term interest rates between the U.S. and euro zone. \par \par \uc2  Ferridge sees the euro stretching its recovery this month and getting closer to parity by the year's end, with a forecast of 99 cents by the end of November. \par \par \uc2  The ECB meets again on Sept. 14, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated following last week's hike that more tightening may be needed to stem inflation. \par \par \uc2  However, other analysts believe that the euro will once again get sold off after its corrective recovery gets played out. \par \par \uc2  "It will continue to be easy to sell the euro against the dollar," said Ryohei Muramatsu, treasury manager at Commerzbank in Tokyo \par \par \uc2  Muramatsu sees euro weakness continuing across the board, especially against yen as Japanese institutional investors unload more of their euro investments towards the end of the fiscal half at the end of September. \par \par \uc2  "Then, as the Japanese economy recovers, the yen should take over as the main actor in this pairing and the 'euro selling' trend should change into 'yen buying,"' he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan is expected to soon report another strong performance for growth in the second-quarter, and analysts see the yen continuing to strengthen. \par \par \uc2  "Over the next two or three months, Japan's economic recovery will become clearer," said Kazuki Fujita, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Industrial Bank of Japan in Tokyo, who sees the dollar targeting Y105 by the end of the month. However, he said any further moves would be limited by fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Taichi Sakaiya, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Economic Planning Agency said last Friday that the dollar's drop to Y106 wasn't significant, but he warned that a "gradual and continued" rise in the yen would be problematic. \par \par \uc2  With the economic recovery still struggling, however, not all analysts think the yen can continue to gain. \par \par \uc2  Barclays' Foley, said the yen will be underpinned in the short term by the prospect of repatriation of funds by domestic corporates, especially when the Nikkei seems to be faltering. On a three-month horizon, however, she sees the yen weakening to around Y108 as the Japanese recovery remains precarious. \par \par \uc2  "The yen lacks conviction. I don't see it being able to break away," said Foley. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Table of One-Month, Three-Month Forecasts\par                    Sept. 30              Nov. 30\par Kazuki Fujita, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Industrial Bank of Japan, Tokyo\par euro/dollar        $0.8800                $0.8700\par dollar/yen         Y105.00                Y104.00\par euro/yen           Y92.40                 Y90.48\par Ryohei Muramatsu, treasury manager, Commerzbank, Tokyo\par euro/dollar        $0.8600                $0.8500\par dollar/yen         Y104.95   Y103.45\par euro/yen           Y90.26                 Y87.93\par Lee Ferridge, head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy, Rabobank International,\par London\par euro/dollar        $0.9200                $0.9900\par dollar/yen         Y108.00                Y112.00\par euro/yen           Y99.00                 Y111.00\par Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Barclays Capital, London\par euro/dollar        $0.8800                $0.8500\par dollar/yen         Y105.00                Y108.00\par euro/yen           Y92.40                 Y91.80\par David Ethridge, director of capital markets strategy, Standard & Poor's\par MMS, New York\par euro/dollar        $0.9100                $0.9000\par dollar/yen         Y104.00                Y103.00\par euro/dollar        Y94.60                 Y92.70\par Bob Lynch, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, BNP Paribas, New York\par euro/dollar        $0.9200                $0.90