\par\par \b \uc2  Hesistant  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mkt offers ECB  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  opportunity. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2000\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 1 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could find the foreign exchanges more receptive than suspected if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon to halt the euro's slide to record lows. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate rises have proved of little help to the euro, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be the only way for the ECB bank to stop a decline which has seen it shed a quarter of its value against the dollar since its 1999 launch, traders said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Launching its first such euro-buying spree would be a risky strategy for the ECB since it carries the risk of failure and could attract speculative attacks on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the tentativeness with which dealers nudged the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record lows below $0.8840 on Thursday and their subsequent reluctance to keep driving it lower suggests they are less inclined to pick a fight with the ECB right now. \par \par \uc2  "The only thing that will make the market see that there is no sense in continuing to sell the euro is if they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Keith Lewis, manager, treasury markets at American Express Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "They may have to come in at regular intervals to get the message across, but if they do, people will be inclined to go with it. There is always another record low around the corner, but it is getting to the stage where people are thinking do I want to be the one selling at these levels." \par \par \uc2  SOLO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  COULD WORK \par \par \uc2  The ECB's prime mandate is to ensure price stability but the weakness of the euro, which has shed more than 17 percent on broad trade-weighted measures since launch, is seen generating price pressures it cannot ignore. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself cited the consequence of the euro's weakness as one of the reasons for the quarter percentage point rate rise it delivered on Thursday and said it would remain alert to emerging risks to price stability. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone central bankers have made it clear they are not happy with moves in the euro - Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet said on Friday that there was currently "an absolutely flagrant underestimation of the value of the euro." \par \par \uc2  Still, euro zone central bankers, including Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, have said in the past central banks should only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they were certain they would succeed. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a very powerful weapon in their armoury but the problem is that if they use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fail, then they will have a real crisis on their hands," said Mark Cliffe, chief economist at ING Barings in London. \par \par \uc2  Some euro zone central bankers have said in recent months that such success could only be guaranteed with U.S. and Japanese aid. \par \par \uc2  But the foreign exchanges' current hesitancy to drive the euro lower means even solo ECB action could work, traders said. "If the ECB came in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively, I would think the euro had bottomed," said a senior dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  He said he would start stocking up on the euro if this was the case as he expected sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would drive it back closer to the dollar parity level last seen in February. \par \par \uc2  The success with which Japan has kept the yen trapped in a range between 100 and 115 per dollar for a year has shown traders central banks can successfully fight the inclinations of the $1.5-trillion-a-day foreign exchanges on their own. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold yen at least 17 times between June 1999 and April 2000 in its fight to curb industry-crippling strength in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The ECB may have to show equal determination if it is to convince dealers that the euro's lifelong decline is at an end. \par \par \uc2  The window of opportunity to use \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse the euro's 20-month downtrend may also prove a small one as the foreign exchanges will eventually pluck up the courage to batter the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. \par \par \uc2  "As the week comes to an end, we are seeing some of the bravado fade away but we should see another attack on the euro in the next week or two," said Colin Clarkson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Sumitomo Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "People are reluctant to do anything themselves and are waiting for someone to come in and drive it down. When this happens the whole market will follow." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw91098zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
