\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro mired near record lows, eyes U.S. data. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2000\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 1 (Reuters) - The euro on Friday was mired within one percent of the record lows it set against the dollar a day earlier amid lingering fears that higher euro zone interest rates could harm euro zone economic growth. \par \par \uc2  At the same time there was market optimism that the U.S. economy was on course for a soft landing, which would help maintain the attractiveness of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Traders said caution ahead of the key U.S. non-farm payrolls and average earnings data, due at 1230 GMT, coupled with a U.S. holiday on Monday, have so far this session limited the euro's losses against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  However, they added that if the U.S. data reinforced expectations of a soft landing, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably renew its decline. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday the euro hit record lows, but we did bounce back quickly, which is not a surprise because the incentive to aggressively buy dollars is limited ahead of the U.S. data," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI. \par \par \uc2  "But the euro's upside looks limited and the major point is the lack of confidence in the euro and growth prospects in euro zone are seen questionable. If U.S. average earnings come in at or above 0.4 percent and unemployment rate falls below 4.0 percent, that will be positive for the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The euro was languishing at 94.50 yen, within one percent of all-time low around 94 yen set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hovering around $0.8890, down about 24 percent since its launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  It was within one percent of a record low of $0.8837 set on Thursday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark minimum bid rate by 25 basis points to 4.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  The hawkish tone of statement issued by the ECB after the rate move triggered speculation that more euro zone rate hikes were in the pipeline. But this was seen as a euro negative as it raised the prospect of a hard landing in the region, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The (ECB) rate hike doesn't change anything, the reality is the euro has few short-term fans," said Kit Juckes, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  "The appetite of investors is still to buy U.S. assets and to be sceptical about anything in Europe." \par \par \uc2  TRICHET COMMENTS PROVIDE LITTLE INSPIRATION \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet's comments that there is a "flagrant" undervaluation of the euro had little impact on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of U.S. data. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone manufacturing Purchasing Managers' data, which showed that the pace of growth in the region has slowed, was also more or less overlooked by the market. \par \par \uc2  Euro zone August manufacturing PMI fell to 58.6 in August from 59.2 in July, its lowest since February. The Eurozone Prices Index dropped to 67.1 last month, its lowest since December 1999, from 72.0 in July. \par \par \uc2  Traders noted Friday was the deadline for payment for the UMTS mobile phone licences recently auctioned in Germany for 100 billion euros. \par \par \uc2  While the successful bidders should have covered most of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure by now, but dealers still wondered if there might be a last-minute scramble for euros. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was trapped in tight ranges against the yen, largely unmoved by a spate of comments from senior Japanese officials. \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said he was not worried about the yen's recent strength against the dollar. Meanwhile, the Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said BOJ monetary policy continued to support \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic recovery and added that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should be carried out if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was idling around 106.50, stuck to a tight 106.30-80 range so far this session. \par \par \uc2  There was some speculation the dollar might benefit from Citigroup Inc's record 175 billion yen Samurai bond issue since dealers suspected some of the yen would be swapped into dollars. \par \par \uc2  But traders also noted that while several foreign banks had launched Samurai bonds recently, many were using the proceeds to expand their business in Japan, so keeping the yen at home. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw91097br\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
