\par\par \b \uc2  Euro rebounds swiftly amid  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2 153 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2000\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 30 (Reuters) - The euro rebounded from record lows against the yen and three-month lows against the dollar, with its swift gains attributed by traders to wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said such rumours were sweeping through the foreign exchanges just as U.S. trading got underway and had had the most impact on euro/yen. However, they added that they had not seen any concrete flows to back up such talk. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers were all the more sensitive to talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said earlier on Wednesday Japan was ready to take appropriate steps in the foreign exchanges markets if needed. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to 95 yen by 1206 GMT from record lows near 94 yen hit earlier in Europe. It was at $0.8920 , up from three-month troughs near $0.8870 hit earlier. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8u09f0j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
