\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Against Pound, But Ends Higher Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2000\par 14:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar finished mixed Monday, falling against the British pound on strong United Kingdom manufacturing data, but climbing against the yen and recovering from intraday losses to end flat against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday, the pound bought $1.5110, up from $1.5019 late Friday. The euro traded at 90.78 U.S. cents, unchanged from late Friday in New York. The dollar fetched 109.07 yen, up from 108.61 yen late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In the U.K., a greater than expected expansion of manufacturing and industrial production helped give the pound a boost. \par \par \uc2  Manufacturing production rose 0.2% in June and 2.1% on the year, compared with expectations for a 0.1% fall on the month and a 2% rise on the year. Industrial production rose by 0.1% on the month and by 2% on the year. A fall of 0.1% and a rise of 1.7%, respectively, had been expected. \par \par \uc2  The euro briefly broke above 91 cents, reaching an intraday high of 91.11 cents at midday in New York, but then fell back to close flat. \par \par \uc2  In keeping with the euro's unpredictable nature, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little reaction to German industrial output for June, which contracted 3.5% on the month, the first decline in six months, and far more than expectations of a 0.6% drop. \par \par \uc2  Michael McGuinness, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at American Express in New York, said he expects to see the euro trade up to around 92 cents if the U.S. producer price index, due to be released on Friday, offers further evidence that inflation remains in check and the Federal Reserve is likely to remain on hold at its Aug. 22 policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  But Dan Bernzweig, a trader at Bank Leumi in New York, said the lack of dollar sell orders above 91 cents suggests that the euro is "far from oversold" and he added, "the long-term chart range is bearish for the euro." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami repeated Monday that he wants to end the bank's near-zero rate policy as soon as possible. The yen failed to react to the comments, however, as recent stock-market weakness in Tokyo and the bankruptcy of retailer Sogo are expected to stave off any move by BOJ hawks to raise rates at this Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board meeting. \par \par \uc2  Mr. McGuinness of American Express said that even in the unlikely event of a rate boost in Japan, the impact on the yen should be small. He expects the dollar to hold within its range of 107.60 yen to 109.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  Separately, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  books to the public Monday, releasing details of the size and date of its transactions for the first time. In the April to June quarter, the ministry said it conducted one case of dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling 1.385 trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Asia, the Thai baht came under another bout of speculative selling pressure, with the dollar closing above the key psychological level of 41 baht amid renewed concerns about Thailand's political situation. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee ended at its lowest closing level against the dollar for the sixth consecutive session. The rupee has lost 1.5% of its value against the dollar in the past six sessions on dollar-buying by state-owned companies, foreign funds and importers. \par \par \uc2 