\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Up Slightly Vs Yen In Thin Trading \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2000\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- An early speculative attack at the dollar's upside fizzled Monday in Asia, leaving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basically unchanged against the yen in generally thin market conditions. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, managed to eke out minor gains versus the yen from levels in New York late Friday, helping to support the dollar against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was relatively thin as many traders and their customers are absent for summer vacations, some speculators are still active in the market. \par \par \uc2  Those players targeted stop-loss orders at the high-Y108 level, buying the dollar aggressively in the morning. The brief flurry of buying sent the dollar to an intraday high of Y108.88. However, those players dumped their long positions when follow-through buying didn't materialize. As a result, the dollar fell back down to where it started. \par \par \uc2  "Speculators tried to trigger stops on the upside," said a senior Japanese trust bank dealers. "Then they squared their positions and some went short on the upside." \par \par \uc2  After the morning activity, trading slowed in the afternoon. "There aren't any flows and its seems that overseas players aren't really doing anything," said Takeo Suzuki, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. \par \par \uc2  Most traders said they expect the dollar to remain in a narrow range for the rest of the week as the market awaits the Bank of Japan's policy board meeting slated for Friday. \par \par \uc2  Of interest to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  books to the public Monday, releasing details of the size and date of its transactions for the first time ever. \par \par \uc2  In the April-June quarter, the ministry said it conducted one case of dollar-buying yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling Y1.385 trillion. The ministry will release the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted via its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account at the end of each quarter. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y108.67, above Y108.61 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.44 late Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9092, higher than $0.9078 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.9038 in Tokyo Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y99.80, up from Y98.60 in New York late Friday. The euro was at Y98.01 in Tokyo late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Hawkish remarks Monday by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami barely registered in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "If we only look at the real economy, deflationary worries have disappeared," Hayami said in parliamentary testimony. Previously, Hayami had said such worries were on the verge of disappearing. \par \par \uc2  The abolishment of deflationary concerns is the BOJ's stated condition for terminating its zero interest rate policy. In that respect, Hayami's comments appear to give the go-ahead for the BOJ to lift short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  However, most \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said they don't expect the BOJ to change policy when its policy board gathers on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "That's Hayami's own opinion," said the senior trust bank dealer, adding that it's not yet necessarily the majority view of the BOJ policy board. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise petered out below Y109 despite a conspicuous absence of sales interest from Japanese exporters. Offers from those players had been a major a weight on the dollar in recent weeks. The failure to make further gains to the upside was due a lack of demand for dollars rather than a flood of supply, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "In the wake of the weak U.S. employment data, there aren't many people aggressively buying the dollar," the senior trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Data released Friday in the U.S. showed a surprising drop of 108,000 in July nonfarm payrolls, the first decline in over four years. Meanwhile, unemployment was steady at 4.0%. \par \par \uc2  Although overseas players might take a crack at the dollar's upside when London trading gets underway later in the session, most traders said the dollar will likely stick to a narrow band in Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The market is kind of rangish now," Nomura's Suzuki said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is likely to find initial support at Y108.25 and further support at Y107.90. Resistance is expected ahead of Y109.00, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In other trading, the euro rose against the yen on the back of bids from overseas players and got an additional boost after a series of stop-loss buy orders around Y90.70 were triggered, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Still, the euro still remains vulnerable to a downside move. "Technically, the euro is weak and the downside risk is pretty large," the trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Initial support is expected at Y97.40 with further support at Y97.00. \par \par \uc2  Monday afternoon in Tokyo, Sterling was at $1.5031, up from $1.5019 in New York and higher than $1.4958 in