\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market Squares Ahead Of Tankan, July 4 Holiday \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2000\par 17:59\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Major U.S. banks have reported little shortage of liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market early Monday in New York ahead of the July 4 holiday, but expect trading to drop off quickly after London wraps up for the day. \par \par \uc2  With U.S. markets closing at 1 p.m. EDT (1700 GMT) and not reopening until Wednesday, traders have had even greater reason to square positions ahead of the much-awaited release of the tankan survey due at 7:50 p.m. EDT (2350 GMT) Monday night. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously there's a little uncertainty going into the tankan survey," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in New York. "It would normally be quiet going into an announcement. Given the holiday here, we expect to get very thin liquidity." \par \par \uc2  One trader at a major New York bank said volume was down to about a third of normal, but others said liquidity wouldn't dry up until London markets close around 11 a.m. EDT (1500 GMT). \par \par \uc2  The tankan survey is expected to show a 2.7% rise in Japanese business sentiment in June, which could prompt the Bank of Japan to abandon its near-zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  A strong result could push dollar/yen lower, and traders have been liquidating long dollar positions Monday. \par \par \uc2  "I think what we've seen since last night is people paring back dollar longs versus yen," said a trader at a major U.S. bank. "It's an event risk hedge versus the stronger-than-expected tankan." \par \par \uc2  Around noon EDT (1600 GMT), the dollar was at Y105.75, down somewhat from Y105.99 late Friday in New York. The euro was at $0.9530, steady from $0.9534 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say a descent to around Y104.5 or Y104 on the back of a strong tankan could trigger an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ and (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have tended to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on announcement days," said Sinche. "And tankan has been catalyst before for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so I wouldn't rule it out at all." \par \par \uc2  A trader said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come into play if dollar/yen falls to Y104, but he said the market would like to take the cross higher. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Even with tankan coming in better, I don't think the market thinks\par Japan is in a very strong position" economically, he said.\par    -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385;\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw7300glv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
