\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Ex-BOJ Kaku sees rate hike within 3 mos. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deborah Collis \par \uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2000\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 28 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan will likely stop pushing interest rates virtually to zero within the next three months, perhaps as soon as July, former BOJ official Kagehide Kaku said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "I think the chances are very big that the Bank of Japan will change its interest rate policy, leave the zero interest rate policy, at the earliest in July, or else a couple of months from now," Kaku told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  He clarified this timeframe as being within three months, if the BOJ wants to confirm improvement in the economy by examining further household spending data and April-June gross domestic product data, due out in September. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ is in a hurry to change policy, he said, it could do so at the July 17 Policy Board meeting, based on the results of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment, due out next Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The board was meeting as he spoke on Wednesday, but was expected to take no action before the tankan release. \par \par \uc2  Kaku, who as head of statistics and research at the BOJ presented the tankan to the press in the mid-1990s, is now deputy chairman of the Daiwa Institute of Research. \par \par \uc2  He said ending the zero-rate policy, begun as an emergency measure in February 1999, would not have a significant impact on the economy, long-term interest rates or on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where the yen has recently risen on expectations of the first Japanese rate hike in 10 years. \par \par \uc2  Kaku said that as long as the yen does not strengthen beyond 100 to the dollar, the rise would not have a serious impact on the economy, adding that he believed the Tokyo authorities share this view. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading in the mid-105-yen zone on Wednesday. Earlier this week it slipped below 104 yen but recovered partly on concern that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. Kaku said the key point of interest for BOJ policymakers in the coming tankan would be the extent of improvement in capital spending plans for small and mid-sized companies. \par \par \uc2  Top BOJ officials have said that a key to ending rates will be a bit more evidence that conditions surrounding household spending - the biggest chunk of the economy - have stabilised. \par \par \uc2  But Kaku noted that the tankan, a survey of companies, would provide only indirect evidence of this vital aspect of the economy. \par \par \uc2  "The sustainability of the improvement (in the economy) is not confirmed yet," he said. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022, tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6s02srt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
