\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen firms after Japan polls, euro broadly weak. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2000\par 09:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 26 (Reuters) - The yen firmed broadly and rose to one-month highs against the euro on Monday after Japan's ruling coalition managed to retain a slim majority in the weekend elections and removed some political uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  An absence of Japanese monetary authorities' actions to curb the yen's export-crippling strength also prompted dealers to test the officials' resolve, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Unless we see something substantive from MOF/BOJ (the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), in terms of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will try and probe higher levels in the yen," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese poll, which returned the ruling bloc with a sharply reduced majority, was mostly in line with expectations and analysts predicted little change in monetary or fiscal policy as a result, he added. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose as far as 97.08 per euro by 0730 GMT, up nearly one percent from Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  Still, the yen was still below the two-month peaks it had briefly spiked up to against the dollar on Friday as the market looked into the details of the election results and also awaited the U.S. Federal Reserve's two-day monetary policy meeting starting on Tuesday, analysts said. The yen was trading at 104.34/39 per dollar at 0654 GMT, up about a quarter yen from Friday's close but a half yen down Friday's intra-day peaks around 103.90. \par \par \uc2  MARKET STILL WARY OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Speculation about the pain threshold of the Japanese authorities has been keeping the market on its toes after the yen rose past the 105 level against the dollar last week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  That level had previously been seen as the trigger level for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said earlier in the month that the market was aware that dollar/yen would not go beyond that level. \par \par \uc2  "The market focus is now shifting to the policy stance of monetary authorities, but it is unclear which level will trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Taisuke Tanaka, director and gobal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo said. \par \par \uc2  So far, the market has seen only much worn-out rhetoric from the authorities. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda said on Monday the yen's rise at the end of last week did not reflect economic fundamentals and also reiterated that Japan was ready to take appropriate action in the market if needed. \par \par \uc2  The euro was broadly softer as doubts spread about its longer-term recovery. Its failure to extend gains after reaching two-month peaks around $0.97 earlier this month had dented sentiment, which had been on the mend since its recovery from record lows hit in May, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at three-week lows against the dollar just above $0.93 early in Europe. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to conduct its first variable rate repo tender this week, which could put upward pressure on interest rates and offer some support for the euro, analysts said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6q02v9x\par }\pa