\par\par \b \uc2  Yen concedes ground to dollar as BOJ looms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2000\par 09:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 23 (Reuters) - The yen surrendered some of its heavy overnight gains in Tokyo on Friday as investors' appetite for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was blunted by the growing prospect of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ahead of this weekend's general election. \par \par \uc2  But the retreat was modest and traders pointed out that there still remained stuck in short-yen positions, which have to be bought back, implying more upward pressure on the yen to come. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  were quick to fire a verbal broadside at the market, arguing a rise in the yen was unwarranted by fundamentals and warning of direct action to correct it. \par \par \uc2  The words carried more than their usual weight since the dollar had sundered 105.00 yen overnight, a key support level previously singled out by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa as a pain threshold for authorities. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the economies of the United States, Japan, and Europe, there is no reason for the yen's strength," Haruhiko Kuroda, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The remarks helped the dollar to recover some ground against the yen from its overnight low of 104.12. It was quoted at 104.63/68 yen at 0545 GMT in late afternoon trade. It moved in 104.54-104.90 yen range. \par \par \uc2  "The overseas accounts have been cutting short positions in the yen against dollar and euro, but there remain some more bad (yen) shorts to be rinsed from the market," said Toshihiko Masaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at ABN AMRO Bank N.V.'s Tokyo branch. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that with BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a real possibility, short holders were waiting for the yen to fall back before bailing out of their positions. \par \par \uc2  Weighing in the yen's favour is the perception that Japan's economy is recovering, which in turn will force the BOJ to raise interest rates for the first time in a decade. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ adopted the hyper-loose credit stance 16 months ago to stave off deflation and help the economy recover, but the bank considers the policy "abnormal" and has been laying the groundwork to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  A fresh newspaper survey on Friday showed Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori's three-way ruling bloc looked set to win a stable majority in parliament's powerful Lower House and his Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) to fare well in Sunday's poll despite his own tattered popularity. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts believe the coalition government will keep implementing economic stimulus and will not be able to keep Japan's mushrooming public debt in check, suggesting a weaker yen. But others disagree. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the coalition wins, the emphasis on Japan's structural reform will not fade away, particularly if the current LDP secretary general (Koichi) Kato gets an important post," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  The market is keenly awaiting the BOJ's key "tankan" business sentiment survey due on July 4 which many expect will justify the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to abort its ultra-easy credit policy. \par \par \uc2  Many economists forecast that the next tankan should confirm large corporate profit rebounds, more confident sales growth projections and higher capital spending plans, as well as steady improvements in the business sentiment of small firms. \par \par \uc2  If the tankan shows small companies are catching up with major firms, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market expects the BOJ to decide to raise the key call rate target to 0.25 percent from zero at its Policy Baord meeting on July 17. \par \par \uc2  All of Wall Street's primary dealers, the institutions that deal directly with the Fed in the money market, expect the 6.50 percent federal funds rate to be kept steady at a two-day meeting of the FOMC starting on June 27. \par \par \uc2  For nearly a year, the Fed has repeatedly raised interest rates to help slow robust growth and now a string of softer statistics suggest that medicine is taking effect. \par \par \uc2  With most market focus on the yen, the euro was content to stay on the sidelines during Tokyo hours. \par \par \uc2  The euro shed a full cent overnight to a three-week trough of $0.9345. It did edge up to $0.9377 in early trading. It was quoted at $0.9365/70 at 0550 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The air of caution helped the euro up from a deep low of 97.50 yen overnight to stand at 97.96/98.06 yen. But that remained a long way from Thursday's late Tokyo level of 99.36 yen and was down almost five percent from the 103.00 high seen just a week ago. Implied volatility in dollar/yen and euro/yen that surged higher on Thursday stayed firmer on Friday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen implied volatility stayed firmer at 13.0/13.5 percent, compared with 13.1/13.4 percent in late New York on Thursday. One-month euro/yen implied volatility popped up to 15.0/15.45 percent, compared with 14.8/15.25 percent in late New York on Thursday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0605 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   1999 \par \par \uc2  Cls \par \par \uc2  22 Jun. \par \par \uc2  ----------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2  Euro/dlr   0.9368   0.9357   +0.12    -6.97   1.0070 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/yen    104.61   104.53   +0.08    +2.35   102.21 \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen   98.01    97.88   +0.13    -4.94   103.10 \par \par \uc2  Dlr/swiss  1.6534   1.6543   -0.05    +3.97   1.5902 \par \par \uc2  Stg/dlr    1.5104   1.5124   -0.13    -6.63   1.6176 \par \par \uc2  Can/dlr    1.4765   1.4760   +0.03    +2.14   1.4455 \par \par \uc2  Aus/dlr    0.5955   0.5965   -0.17    -9.26   0.6563 \par \par \uc2  Euro/swiss 1.5497   1.5485   +0.08    -3.36   1.6036 \par \par \uc2  Euro/stg   0.6199   0.6186   +0.21    -0.64   0.6239. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6n033ki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
