\par\par \b \uc2  Yen resolute as market dares  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2000\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 23 (Reuters) - They yen had to surrender some of its hefty offshore gains on Friday morning as the Tokyo market succumbed to a well-developed fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the retreat was orderly and modest and yen bulls were soon marshalling their forces for another charge at the topside. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  were quick to fire a verbal broadside at the market, arguing a rise in the yen was unwarranted by fundamentals and warning of direct action to correct it. \par \par \uc2  The words carried more than their usual weight since the dollar had sundered 105.00 yen overnight, a key support level previously singled out by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa as a pain threshold for authorities. \par \par \uc2  Dealers heeded the warning in part, taking the dollar back up to 104.60 yen from a two-month low of 104.12 overnight, but the rally was quickly stymied by fresh offers atop 104.80. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said talk of more option positions at 104.00 yen were also tempting speculative dollar sellers. \par \par \uc2  The air of caution helped the euro up from deep lows around 97.50 yen to stand at 97.90. But that remained a long way from Thursday's late Tokyo level of 99.36 yen and was down almost five percent from the 103.00 high seen just a week ago. \par \par \uc2  Neither was the euro in better shape against the dollar, having shed a full cent overnight to a three-week trough of $0.9345. It did edge up to $0.9359 by late morning but dealers reported stiff offers from U.S. corporates at $0.9380. \par \par \uc2  And while the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reined in the yen for the moment, many suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be more open to a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  given it was largely due to expectations of economic recovery and higher interest rates in Japan. \par \par \uc2  "In part the yen is strong because Japanese exporters are doing very well. Japanese companies are gaining market share globally, and as a result of that you're in an environment where the economy can tolerate a higher yen," Jesper Koll, chief economist at Merrill Lynch in Tokyo, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  Sensing this, the market was testing the waters to see exactly how high a yen the ministry would tolerate. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's at least a 50 percent probability the MOF is prepared to let us drift into a higher trading range, a 95-100 yen trading range," he said. Analysts were also revising up the probability of an early policy tightening by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Comments by BOJ Deputy Governor Sakuya Fujiwara on Thursday, construed as hawkish by the market, were ostensibly the trigger for the market to test and finally crush support at 105.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  Once that went, a mountain of options positions were triggered at 105.00, 104.80 and 104.50 yen leading to an avalanche of stop-loss sell orders. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has made it very clear they want to end the emergency zero-rate policy as soon as possible," said Koll, identifying the next "tankan" survey of business sentiment on July 4 as crucial for gleaning clues on the timing of any move. \par \par \uc2  "If the tankan shows small companies are catching up then the argument can be made for rates to be hiked by 25 basis points on July 17," he added. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ Policy Board meets on July 17 and speculation has grown rampant it will move at that meeting. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said it was unfortunate for the euro that speculation of a BOJ move had picked up just when it was technically vulnerable to a correction after a recent steep rally. \par \par \uc2  "The euro made double-tops on both the dollar and the yen, which were clear sell signals," said a European bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "And the market saw shorting euro/yen as a safer bet than dollar/yen since there was much less chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on that cross," he added. \par \par \uc2  Analysts divined bearish omens from the charts on a number of euro crosses, particlularly euro/yen which had shattered Fibonacci support at 97.90 yen. Many were now targetting support at 96.20, and some even talked of a return to its all-time lows around 94.90. \par \par \uc2  The euro also snapped Fibonacci support at $0.9370, which provided further confirmation of a double-top on the daily charts at 97 cents and set up a target of 92 cents in the near term. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6n031dk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
