\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Recoups Early Losses Against Yen, Holds Steady Against Euro \b0\par\par\uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2000\par 14:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar recovered from an earlier drop below 104 yen to move higher Friday, as fears of Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overhadowed speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's near-zero rate policy is nearing an end. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also pared its losses against the euro, with the single euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggling to claw its way back from this week's swoon. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was quoted at 104.72 yen, up from 104.48 yen late Thursday in New York. The dollar hit a new nine-week low of 103.93 yen at 8:30 a.m. EDT before rebounding. The euro traded at 93.65 U.S. cents, little changed from 93.60 cents late Thursday. The British pound fetched $1.5032, down from $1.5118 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar's gains came mostly from traders squaring up positions in thin market conditions ahead of the Japanese elections Sunday. Also, the BOJ is considered likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring the yen back down once the elections are out of the way. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko Kuroda warned Friday that officials are "prepared to take action if necessary." \par \par \uc2  The yen had been helped recently by a slowdown in outflows from Japan and a bullish view on Japan's economic recovery. As Japan struggles to mount a lasting economic recovery, the BOJ has maintained a loose monetary policy, worrying that an overly strong yen might harm Japanese exports. However, recent statements by Minstry of Finance officials and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have suggested that the easy-money rate policy could soon be over. \par \par \uc2  Tony Norfield, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN-Amro in London, said the BOJ is more likely to wait until the dollar hits 102 yen -- which could happen early next week -- before mounting an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last time the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was April 17, when the dollar sank to 103.30 yen. \par \par \uc2  With little market-moving news Friday, traders said the euro's fleeting push above 94 cents was mostly technical and didn't necessarily represent a turnaround from recent weakness in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has lost nearly three cents since last Friday, when it traded at 96.50 cents. \par \par \uc2  The euro's failure to rally earlier this week on the back of a strong German Ifo index and other favorable figures has resulted in many traders selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "There seems to be a convergence in growth and interest rates" between the U.S. and euro zone, said Robert Houck, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis. "All of that is obviously very euro favorable. Because the euro hasn't rallied, people are throwing in the towel and saying the euro's time has't come yet." \par \par \uc2  Traders are also waiting to see what the Federal Reserve will do at its policy meeting next week. Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely expected to hold rates steady when it concludes a two-day meeting Wednesday, economists are looking ahead to data to be released in July to determine if the Fed will resume its string of monetary tightenings at its Aug. 22 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The Fed has raised rates six times, for a total of 1.75 percentage points, since last June in an effort to slow the economy and prevent inflation from creeping in. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, Mexico's peso continued to weaken on higher-than-expected inflation data and pre-election jitters, but pared earlier steep losses. In Mexico City, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 9.9380 per dollar, weaker from its Thursday close of 9.9100 per dollar. The dollar met resistance, however, after crossing the psychological threshold of 10 pesos in early transactions. \par \par \uc2  Mexico's consumer price index rose 0.38% in the first two weeks of June, above market expectations. \par \par \uc2  Volatility is expected to continue next week, the last week before the July 2 presidential vote. Recent polls indicate that both the ruling party and opposition party candidates are in a statistical dead heat. \par \par \uc2  Market participants are also keeping an eye on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although the Fed is not expected to raise interest rates next week, it could give some sign of what to expect in August. \par \par \uc2  Higher U.S. interest rates pressure the peso by making dollar-denominated securities more attractive, and by slowing down economic growth in the U.S., Mexico's chief trading partner. \par \par \uc2  (Compiled from Dow Jones Newswires and other sources) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw6n01huw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
