\par\par \b \uc2  Euro losing ground as sentiment swings yen's way. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2000\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 22 (Reuters) - The euro nursed heavy losses on Thursday after an offshore rout, with the yen the main gainer on scaled-up expectations of an economic revival in Japan. Led by a selling spree against the yen, the euro slid overnight on the dollar, the Swiss franc and sterling. Meanwhile, the yen swept the board, rising to a three-week high on the euro and gaining almost two percent on the pound. Only against the dollar was it restrained, as the market worried about the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In late morning trade the euro was floundering at 99.56 yen, having fallen over half a yen to a 99.47 low overnight and breaching important psychological support at 100.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro was lying at $0.9458 after skidding to $0.9442 in New York from $0.9520 in Tokyo on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The market initially shied away from pushing the yen higher on the dollar, but as the morning progressed selling from U.S. investment banks dragged the dollar down to 105.26 yen from 105.55 late in New York. \par \par \uc2  "Prospects for Japan's economic recovery have buoyed sentiment, fuelling a yen-buying spree" said an interbank dealer. Dealers said also aiding the yen was an easing of political uncertainty ahead of Japan's general election on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Newspaper polls show the ruling Liberal Democratic Party coalition should gain a stable majority, meaning Japan's economic policy would not change drastically. \par \par \uc2  Japan's firmer stock market also served as a psychological support for the yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I think investors are more aware Japan is a rather attractive place to invest," said one dealer, pointing to potential yen demand among foreigners. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei stock average was up for the fourth day in a row to stand 135.86 points higher at 17,345.94 by midday, its best level in five weeks. \par \par \uc2  The improvement in the Nikkei and the economy in general has fed speculation the Bank of Japan would soon end its 16-month old zero-interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Deputy Governor Sakuya Fujiwara, added to the recent string of comments by BOJ officials, saying an end to deflation fears was near, but more monitoring was needed. \par \par \uc2  One trader pointed out that demand for the yen is likely remain firm, given Japan's huge trade surplus with the United States. The latest U.S. data showed the surplus hit a record $7.3 billion in April, implying underlying demand for yen. \par \par \uc2  For the euro, the market has had to scale back its expectations for interest rate rises after ECB President Wim Duisenberg said earlier this week the bank would keep rates on hold for some months to come. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left rates unchanged at its policy meeting on Wednesday as expected. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers said the euro's weakness came more from a correction from a two-month long rally. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro's repeated failure to climb above $0.9700 had dampened expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would extend gains to near parity. The euro ceded ground to the Swiss franc to a near one-month low at 1.5500 francs. The euro was last lower against the Swiss franc when it hit 1.5465 on May 17. Below this level comes the euro's all-time low of 1.5396 set on May 4. \par \par \uc2  Against sterling the euro was quoted at 0.6292/98 pence, near a two-week low. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0241 GMT. All