\par\par \b \uc2  Citibank Establishes Long Dlr/Yen Position At Y104.40 \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2000\par 22:49\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar's dive through Y105 for the first time in two months Thursday presents a great  opportunity for investors to establish long dollar/yen positions, according to Citigroup \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar's sharp decline to support around Y104.10 early in afternoon trading - the first time it has  seen that level since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Y103.30 on April 17 - Citiviews established a long dollar/yen position at Y104.40. \par \par \uc2  "We find this an excellent opportunity to establish long dollar/yen exposure," said Citiviews in a press release. \par \par \uc2  And there may be only a small window for investors to take advantage of the bargain on dollars before the Bank of Japan steps in, Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York, told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's a reasonable chance the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan will come in tonight," said Sinche. "It's a medium-term entry level that I'm not sure  will be there tomorrow." \par \par \uc2  With elections coming up this weekend, Japanese officials likely will want to quell any instability in yen, he said. \par \par \uc2  Sinche said a "more reasonable" level for dollar/yen  is the Y105-Y110 range it has traded in since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April. \par \par \uc2  The elections are also unlikely to be yen-supportive, according to Citiviews. While the ruling Liberal Democrat Party coalition looks likely to stay in power, there are questions about the direction of fiscal policy in the future. \par \par \uc2  Fiscal conservative Koichi Kato is considered a leading challenger to Prime Minister Yoshiro Mori, and his  likely focus on reform could stall Japan's recovery and weigh on the yen, said Sinche. \par \par \uc2  Also, market sentiment may have gone too far in its assumption that the Federal Reserve is easing back on its monetary policy, as well as in the viewpoint that the BOJ will soon be abandoning its near-zero interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  With the euro/yen market pricing in 30 basis points in  BOJ tightening by the year end, there is downside risk for yen, according to Citiviews. The bank's economists view market expectations of rate hikes as being at the high end of potential outcomes. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the market has gotten itself long euro/yen again," said Sinche. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said the real test for determining a more reasonable level for\par dollar/yen will be the next batch of Japanese economic data, including\par the Tankan report on July 4.\par    -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385;\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1140 4220 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw6m00fj4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
