\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options - Euro/dlr drop sparks little vol change. \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2000\par 22:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, June 21 (Reuters) - Implied volatility in euro/dollar options stayed relatively flat on Wednesday despite the euro's steep erosion to a two-week low versus the dollar, while dollar/yen vols firmed as that cross neared key support. \par \par \uc2  "(Euro) vols really didn't change that much relative to yesterday. They stayed pretty consistent," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trader said. \par \par \uc2  The euro slumped sharply versus the dollar after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held rates steady on Wednesday, as expected, one day after ECB President Wim Duisenberg told a European Parliament committee rates were on hold for months. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell below 95.00 cents to 94.31 cents and also broke 100 yen to reach 99.50 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro dollar implied volatility was near 12.1/12.4 percent late on Wednesday, about the same as Tuesday's close. One-year euro volatility firmed to 12.6/12.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  "We will probably get a kick (up in volatility) if we get an upside breakout, but vols may be a little softer if the euro continues to break to the downside," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  If the euro holds this area for a day or two, then volatility will almost certainly come off a bit, traders said. \par \par \uc2  One-year euro volatility also softened a bit, but the steep decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reflected the most in the risk reversal. The one-month risk reversal continued to favour euro calls but came in to about flat/0.2 from about 0.3/0.5. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen held the key 105 spot level below which some traders expect to find stops, but jitters over approaching that perceived barrier put a bid in volatility, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Yen strength has been capped recently by the expectation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise against the dollar if dollar/yen breaks below 105. \par \par \uc2  A break lower in dollar/yen would likely spark higher implied volatility, but a failure at that spot level could crush volatility, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "I am not sure if they are buying volatility, but they are raising the offers a little bit," one strategist said. "No question, if dollar/yen closes below 105 then vols will get a heck of a lot higher." \par \par \uc2  One-month dollar/yen implied volatility firmed to 12.2/12.5 percent from just below 12.0 percent in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  One-year dollar/yen implied volatility firmed to a mid of about 13.7 percent from 13.4 percent and the one-month risk reversal premium favouring yen calls rose to 2.4/2.7. ((Chicago Derivatives Desk(312)408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6l036ec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
