\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro fails to shine despite strong Ifo release. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2000\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 20 (Reuters) - The euro rose briefly against the dollar after Germany's key Ifo business climate index came out stronger than expected on Tuesday, but its gains were short-lived as the reading was weaker than had been rumoured. \par \par \uc2  The index surged to 102.1 in May from 101.2 in April, outstripping average market forecast for a rise to 101.4. \par \par \uc2  But the impact on the euro was limited as speculation that it could come in as high as 102.7 had begun to circulate in the foreign exchanges at least an hour before the report was scheduled to be released. \par \par \uc2  "The reaction in the euro is a bit misleading," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very strong number and the sell-off since then is disappointing but results from the fact that the market had priced in ridiculously high numbers. Looking forward the Ifo has got to be seen as euro positive." \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had begun to advance as talk of a strong number swirled around the market, gained about 0.15 cents to hit the day's highs of $0.9621 after the Ifo report but was back at $0.9570 by 0950 GMT. That compares with the two-month highs near $0.97 hit on June 8 which were approached on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day the euro had been offered some support by Italian gross domestic product data for the first quarter of 2000. The data showed a 1.0 percent quarter-on-quarter increase, the biggest such increase since the second quarter of 1997. \par \par \uc2  The euro showed little reaction to European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, who said the rise in oil prices in May points to renewed short term inflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg added that it would be a "disaster" to relax the ECB's two percent inflation target and said he was happy that the euro's slide from its January 1999 peaks seemed to have stopped and partially reversed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers await the release of U.S. April trade data at 1230 GMT, with expectations of a slight narrowing in March's record $30.18 billion deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The risk is that they (the U.S. trade data) are disappointing and that we might see something around $30 billion again," said Ferridge at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  "The market does not normally react too much to trade numbers, but I wouldn't be surprised to see the euro back up above $0.96 if we do see a big trade deficit again." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is due to speak at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  YEN FIRM AS AMID ROSIER OUTLOOK FOR JAPAN \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the yen kept a upper hand across the board as the outlook for Japan's economic looked set to improve, although its gains were capped by fears the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen had benefitted by a momnthly report in which the Economic Planning Agency (EPA) upgraded its assessment of Japan's economy earlier on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Demand for yen was also stimulated by hawkish comments from Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, who said the economy was clearly moving towards an end of Japan's ultra-loose interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  Such remarks spurred expectations that the BOJ might hike rates already at its July meeting. \par \par \uc2  "In general the overall downtrend in dollar/yen completely makes sense," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen started moving lower yesterday and there have been more discussions about ending the zero interest rate policy, with the EPA coming out with a more upbeat view of economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  But market expectations of a near term hike were tempered after EPA Minister Taichi Sakaiya said July would be too soon to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  By 0950 GMT the yen was testing 1-1/2 week peaks around 105.50 against the dollar it hit earlier in the day. The yen had got to around 105 earlier this month but has not been below that level since mid-April. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the yen had firmed more than 0.5 percent on the day to around 100.95 . \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 020 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6k03bpj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
