\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro extends losses, fails to benefit from Ifo. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2000\par 14:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 20 (Reuters) - The euro continued to struggle against the dollar on Tuesday, as investors trimmed some long euro positions given the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inability to capitalise on a strong German business survey. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in European trade, the euro had risen briefly versus the dollar after Germany's Ifo business climate index for May outstripped market expectations, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains were short-lived as the reading was weaker than had been rumoured. \par \par \uc2  Although the Ifo survey and strong Italian gross domestic data for the first quarter of 2000 painted a picture of improving euro zone economic conditions, analysts said the euro was lacking the energy to extend a rally started in mid-May. \par \par \uc2  "The data coming through are strong. I can't see any reason for the euro to go down," Glenn Davies, chief economist at Credit Lyonnais in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's more a question that the euro has moved up a long way and just seems to be a bit exhausted at the moment." \par \par \uc2  The Ifo business climate index for west Germany surged to a fresh nine-year high of 102.1 in May from 101.2 in April, far above market expectations for a rise to 101.4. \par \par \uc2  The index is Germany's most closely-watched leading indicator, but the impact on the euro was limited as speculation that it could come in as high as 102.7 had begun to circulate in the foreign exchanges at least an hour before its release. \par \par \uc2  "The reaction in the euro is a bit misleading," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very strong number and the sell-off since then is disappointing but results from the fact that the market had priced in ridiculously high numbers. Looking forward the Ifo has got to be seen as euro positive." \par \par \uc2  Italian gross domestic product data for the first quarter of 2000 showed a 1.0 percent quarter-on-quarter increase, the biggest such rise since the second quarter of 1997. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which had begun to advance as talk of a strong number swirled around the market, gained about 0.15 cents to hit the day's highs of $0.9621 after the Ifo report but was back at $0.9540 by 1200 GMT. \par \par \uc2  That compares with a two-month high near $0.97 hit on June 8 after a three-week rally from a record lows near $0.8845. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro was put under further pressure after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg indicated that euro zone interest rates were unlikely to change for months. \par \par \uc2  "Duisenberg today confirmed that rates are going to be on hold perhaps until September, which is what (Bundesbank President Ernst) Welteke mentioned last week," said Davies at Credit Lyonnais. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is not getting any push from that." \par \par \uc2  Dealers are awaiting U.S. April trade data at 1230 GMT, with expectations of a slight narrowing in March's record $30.18 billion deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The risk is that they (the U.S. trade data) are disappointing and that we might see something around $30 billion again," said Ferridge at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  "The market does not normally react too much to trade numbers, but I wouldn't be surprised to see the euro back up above $0.96 if we do see a big trade deficit again." \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers is due to speak at 1800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  YEN KEEPS UPPER HAND ON JAPAN'S OUTLOOK \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the yen held broadly firm as the outlook for Japan's economy looked set to improve. But its gains were capped by fears the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen had benefitted from a monthly report in which the Economic Planning Agency (EPA) upgraded its assessment of Japan's economy earlier on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also bolstered after Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said the economy was clearly moving towards an end of Japan's ultra-loose interest rate policy, spurring talk that the BOJ might hike rates already at its July meeting. \par \par \uc2  "In general the overall downtrend in dollar/yen completely makes sense," said Tony Norfield, head of global treasury research at ABN AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen started moving lower yesterday and there have been more discussions about ending the zero interest rate policy, with the EPA coming out with a more upbeat view of economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  But market expectations of a near term hike were tempered after EPA Minister Taichi Sakaiya said July would be too soon to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  By 1200 GMT the yen was within reach of earlier 1-1/2 week peaks around 105.40 against the dollar. Against the euro, the yen was testing three-week highs around 100.75. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6k032n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
