\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Japan Kuroda says euro too weak, especially vs yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2000\par 08:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 15 (Reuters) - Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said on Thursday that global economic trends argue for a stronger euro but no further rises for the yen. \par \par \uc2  The vice finance minister for international affairs recited Group of Seven gospel that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates should reflect economic fundamentals, but said they do not always oblige. \par \par \uc2  "If the Japanese economy grows one percent, the U.S. economy grows 4.0-5.0 percent and the euro area grows 3.0-4.0 percent, there is no rationale for the yen to appreciate," Kuroda told a news conference at the Foreign Correspondents Club of Japan. \par \par \uc2  He expressed confidence that Japan's economy can grow one percent this year, while saying the potential growth rate of the red-hot U.S. economy may be 3.5-4.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "certainly undervalued" with much room to rebound, he said, adding that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was weak against the dollar but particularly against the yen. \par \par \uc2  European authorities have also expressed concern about the euro's weakness and they believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should recover, he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had scant reaction to Kuroda's comments, which reflect his widely known views. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the euro was changing hands around $0.96, compared with its mid-May record low of $0.8845, and at 102.10 yen, against all-time lows around 94.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro has recovered strongly from mid-May record lows against the dollar and yen, helped in part by the apparent U.S. slowdown and by an aggressive interest rate hike last week by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  'NO TARGET ZONES' \par \par \uc2  "The G7 countries have been engaged in trying to make exchange rates reflect economic fundamentals as much as possible, and if necessary to cooperate in exchange rate matters," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  But he declined to tip his hand about any possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying one cannot predict the situations in which G7 members would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates up or down. \par \par \uc2  "If it were possible, then we would virtually be in some sort of target zone or some sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilisation agreement," Kuroda said. "But we have not such ranges or zones or targets, or anything like that, among G7 countries." \par \par \uc2  On the yen, a nagging concern for the Japanese economy as it claws its way out of its worst postwar downturn, Kuroda said: "I'm not advocating a stronger yen or a weaker yen. Rather, I would advocate exchange rates that reflect economic fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  By contrast, Kuroda's boss, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa, has made clear he does not want the dollar tumbling below 100 yen and signalled strongly that the 105.00 yen in a critical line. \par \par \uc2  Evidence of slowing U.S. growth briefly pushed the dollar down last week toward 105.00 yen, but \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's implicit threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  there have helped it bounce to around the mid-106-yen level where it was trading on Thursday. The dollar has zigzagged broadly in a 103-110 yen range since early April, when Tokyo last sold yen in the market, making a splash by buying around $13 billion in just one day. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen can inflame lingering deflationary pressure on the fragile Japanese economy and it makes exports - still a key crutch for the recovery - less competitive. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda agreed that Japan has been more active in the markets in the past several years - which have seen the yen veer between 80 and 140 to the dollar since 1995. \par \par \uc2  But, he said: "In the last 10 years or 20 years, during which the so-called floating exchange rate regime has prevailed, I do not think history shows that Japan is particularly, distinctively, more active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market than other G7 countries." ((With reporting by Wayne Cole and Yoko Nishikawa Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6f03ocw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
