\par\par \b \uc2  Yen well primed for end of Japan zero rate policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2000\par 17:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 12 (Reuters) - The yen should not shoot higher if Japan ends its zero interest rate policy as the move has been well flagged to the market and the economy shows clear signs of recovery, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Speculation the BOJ could end its 16-month old policy of keeping rates virtually at zero has surfaced regularly since BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami stunned markets in April by warning that the time could be ripe to end the regime. \par \par \uc2  The resilience shown by the yen on Monday to the BOJ's majority vote decision to leave its zero rate policy intact was attributed to expectations that a move would come well before the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  While an eventual tightening was seen bolstering the yen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already primed for such a rate move, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is now expecting this move within the next six months and it won't be a huge shock, particularly if it is a 25 basis point move," said Steven Saywell, treasury analyst at Fuji Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "This is the whole strategy behind Hayami warning the market on April 12. We have now seen from the minutes of the monetary policy meeting just preceding that announcement that it was a planned strategy and is coordinated by him and the Bank of Japan." \par \par \uc2  The yen rallied 1.5 percent on the day against the dollar after Hayami's comments and extended its gains by another two percent within days. \par \par \uc2  However, it has ceded those gains as the market has become used to the idea of higher Japanese rates and the dollar is trading back around 106.50 yen , pretty much where it was before Hayami's comments. \par \par \uc2  Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in London said a change in Japan's zero rate policy during the third quarter was discounted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The impact on the yen of a shift in Japanese monetary policy was seen all the more muted given interest in so-called yen carry trades has been running at a low ebb this year. \par \par \uc2  Such trades involve borrowing yen at rock-bottom rates and using the proceeds to buy higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Unwinding such trades generates demand for yen. \par \par \uc2  Piron said uncertainty about how quickly, if at all, the first tightening in monetary policy would be followed up by further hikes would likely limit yen gains. \par \par \uc2  "The abandoning of the zero interest rate policy should be seen very much as a one-off - it is the end of an emergency policy, not the beginning of a tightening cycle," said Piron. \par \par \uc2  GROWTH THE KEY TO BIGGER YEN RALLY \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the BOJ would likely want to see further evidence that economic recovery in Japan was well established before committing to higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Still, only if the timing of the rate hike coincided with a better-than-expected upturn in the Japanese economy would the yen be prone to significant strengthening, said Piron at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "We would emphasise the progress being made in terms of productivity and corporate profitability in Japan and accompanying that is the regional aspect of growth, with demand in the region picking up," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If the rate hike were to take place in that context then a stronger yen would be potentially possible." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said there could be a bigger reaction in the foreign exchanges if the market saw an increase in rates as signalling a sea change in Japan's monetary policy, and began to look for further rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  "When the rate does finally begin to move there will be some panic because it will not be possible to determine at that point just how far yen rates will rise in the course of the cycle," said Stephen Lewis, chief economist at Monument Derivatives. \par \par \uc2  "It is partly because of that panic that we will see a rise in the dollar/yen exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  He said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a rise in the yen was unlikely to deter the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from pushing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has been spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least 17 times since June 1999 to stem the rise of the yen and has made a minimum of four forays into the foreign exchanges this year. \par \par \uc2  Lewis at Monument Derivatives said while fears Japan would try to cap any rise in the yen could deter some from buying yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely only occur for a fairly limited time. \par \par \uc2  "There is a limit to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if there is such a major shift in the monetary policy of the BOJ, it is not likely that it will be able to counter fully the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  consequences of that through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen could well get through 100, maybe to the 95-100 yen range, on the first 25 basis point increase in Japanese interest rates." \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 20 7542 7792, fax +44 20 7542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6c03qmj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s