\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen loses shine as Japan GDP dents optimism. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2000\par 12:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 9 (Reuters) - The yen slipped against the dollar and euro on Friday as it was hit by weaker-than-expected first quarter Japanese economic growth and wider interest rate differentials between the euro zone and Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's gross domestic product grew 2.4 percent in the January-March quarter, the strongest growth in four years, but weaker than a median forecast of 2.9 percent in a Reuters poll of economists. \par \par \uc2  For the fiscal year ended in March, GDP rose 0.5 percent, falling just short of the government's target of 0.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 50 point rate hike on Thursday was also pressuring the yen as the euro zone and Japan rate gap widened in favour of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The yen is being sold from overbought positions because earlier this week people got a little over optimistic about Japanese growth prospects," said a trader at a German bank. \par \par \uc2  "The rate differential is also playing a part. Some investors like to use cheap yen borrowing rates to play on the interest rate differential view because it is still unclear when Japan would end its zero-rate policy and even if Japan raised rates by 25 basis points, it's relatively small." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was hovering just below a day's high of 106.72 yen, up about 1.5 percent from its seven-week low of 104.98 set on Wednesday. The euro rallied about a yen on the day to peak around 102 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The numbers were not too bad, but the yen didn't benefit, because after two quarters of negative growth the market wanted a clear sign that Japan is out of technical recession," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said capital spending and personal consumption were above government expectations, and the government may revise upward its 1.0 percent growth target for the fiscal 2000/01 year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was deriving support from wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's industry-crippling upside. \par \par \uc2  Senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda and his deputy Zembei Mizoguchi repeated on Friday that Japan was ready to act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if needed. \par \par \uc2  EURO SUFFERS AS ECB SEEN STIFLING GROWTH \par \par \uc2  While the euro managed to benefit against the yen from the ECB's rate rise, it was struggling 1-1/2 cents below two-month high of $0.9701 set in the immediate aftermath of the ECB's decision to raise its refinancing rate to 4.25 percent and switch to variable rate money market auctions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they were concerned that the rate rise might choke off burgeoning euro-zone growth and such worries were highlighted by the Portuguese Prime Minister Antonio Guterres who said, late on Thursday, the rate hike was unnecessary and would not help economic growth in Europe. \par \par \uc2  General profit taking by U.S. investors, who had been aggressively buying the euro ahead of the ECB, was also cited as another factor behind the euro's pull back. \par \par \uc2  The euro had risen nearly 10 percent from its record lows around $0.8840 hit on May 19 and racked up two-percent gains on Thursday alone. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday was a case of buy the rumour and sell the fact. Europe bought the euro at higher levels on the back of the ECB rate rise, but the U.S., who had been buying the euro for the last few days took the chance to lock in profits," Phil Roberts, technical analyst at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  "Given they had been the dominant centre in recent days, it was not surprising that the euro therefore came off." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the euro's recent gains against the dollar were also being questioned because the jury is out on whether the U.S. economy was slowing down enough to justify the U.S. Federal Reserve leaving rates on hold this month. \par \par \uc2  Traders said U.S. producer prices data, scheduled for release at 1230 GMT, should be closely watched. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still in a position where it needs more information," said Gerard Gardner, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. REUTERS. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6903ugf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
