\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Asian shares meander down, ignore U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jane Nelson \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2000\par 09:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 8 (Reuters) - Asian share markets meandered generally lower on Thursday as the urge to consolidate and nerves ahead of upcoming U.S. data superceded any positive vibes from Wall Street's overnight strength. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar also spent the day drifting with fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  preventing it from succumbing to a downward tendency. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei 225 average slipped 0.82 percent to close at 17,004.34, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng was trading 0.12 percent down at 15,880.52 at 0600 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very quiet," said Alan Pau, associate director at South China Securities in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are digesting the nearly 2,000 point recent gain in the index and they are waiting for the U.S. producer price index on Friday which will give another indication on whether the U.S. economy is softening." \par \par \uc2  Investors are hoping for further evidence of a U.S. economic slowdown which would indicate there was no need for further U.S. interest rate rises, which also influence local rates. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Wall Street chose to stick with the slowdown view, ignoring warnings from U.S. central bankers about how convincing the recent signs of a slowdown really were. \par \par \uc2  Instead, it switched its attention to corporate news and the end of uncertainty about Microsoft ahead of its long-awaited antitrust case ruling. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq composite index gained 82.89 points or 2.21 percent to close at 3,839.26 and the Dow Jones industrial average rose 0.72 percent to 10,812.86. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street's strength was expected to boost U.K. and European shares when markets there opened later on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "From London's close last night the Dow was up quite strongly and we saw quite a few buyers of the FTSE last night," said one dealer in London. \par \par \uc2  ASIA TRADE MIXED AS CONFUSION REIGNS \par \par \uc2  Australia and New Zealand followed Wall Street's lead. The S&P/ASX 200 index closed at 3,126.7, up 0.45 percent, while New Zealand shares gained 0.33 percent to 2,054.54. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Singapore and India also lifted. The Straits Times Index was up 0.59 percent at 2,046.91 by 0600 GMT and Bombay leapt 2.83 percent to 4,735.16 at the same time. But Korea plunged when profit taking took hold in late trade. The KOSPI lost 2.73 percent to finish at 800.11. \par \par \uc2  "The KOSPI gained about 25 percent during the past six trading days and some feel it's time to cash out," said Sohn Jae-hwa, an analyst at Hanjin Investment and Securities in Korea. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan eased 0.52 percent to end at 9,067.88 and Malaysia extended Wednesday's substantial losses, moving 2.18 percent lower to 836.59 at 0600 GMT. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR BEARISH BUT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  TALK SUPPORTS \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was confined to tight ranges of 105.43 to 105.79 yen on Thursday, not far off a seven-week low. \par \par \uc2  Hints from Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa about possible yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downside, while a belief that Japanese gross domestic product data, due on Friday would show a clear recovery limited its topside, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Miyazawa's remarks had a psychological impact on the market, making operators cautious about selling the dollar aggressively below 105 yen," one Japanese trust bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "But the market will challenge the Bank of Japan and sell the dollar aggressively if the GDP figures show convincing strength in the Japanese economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  The yen's run to a seven-week high of 105.03 yen late on Wednesday was abruptly halted after Miyazawa indicated that 105 yen would be a strong support level. \par \par \uc2  At 0600 GMT, the dollar stood at 105.66/68 yen, compared with 105.32 yen in the U.S. market late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was holding slightly under $0.96, on expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) will raise key short-term interest rates by at least 25 basis points. \par \par \uc2  It was trading at $0.9588/91 and 101.36/41 yen at 0600 GMT, compared with $0.9615 and 101.43 yen in late U.S. trade. REUTERS. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw68041fy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
