\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Advances As Euro's Rally Following ECB Rate Boost Fizzles \b0\par\par\uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2000\par 14:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar finished higher against its major counterparts Thursday, as the euro couldn't hold gains that followed a larger-than-expected rate boost by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The ECB raised its benchmark interest rate by a half-percentage point to 4.25%, exceeding expectations of a quarter-point rise. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the euro traded at 95.59 U.S. cents, down from 96.17 U.S. cents late Wednesday in New York. Following announcement of the ECB's rate increase, the euro rallied to 97.01 U.S. cents, its highest level since April 5, but then dropped as low as 95.11 cents on profit-taking. The British pound traded at $1.5102, down sharply from $1.5221 late Wednesday. The dollar was quoted at 105.98 yen, up from 105.54 yen late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Euro bullishness turned into "buy the rumor, sell the fact" sentiment, as investors weighed the rate boost's potential negative effects on euro-zone growth, said Jordan Rosner, chief economist at Optima Investment Research in Chicago. \par \par \uc2  Also, after such a big increase, Mr. Rosner added, "the thinking now is that it may be some time before they move again" to raise rates. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, ECB President Wim Duisenberg cited the rate hike as a proactive move to stem growing inflationary pressures, and said it "would clear the horizon for some time to come." \par \par \uc2  The euro's drop Thursday indicates that the recent upward trend might be capped at 97 cents for some time, said John Cholakis, a dealer at Dai-Ichi Kangyo Bank in New York. He said that if the euro closes below 95.50 cents, "it could try to hit new all-time lows." \par \par \uc2  Patrick Brodie, assistant vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo Bank in New York, said a break below strong support at 94.90 cents could send the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's increase in its main lending rate matched the U.S. Federal Reserve's half-point rate boost in May, which pushed the federal-funds rate target to 6.5%, its highest level in nine years. \par \par \uc2  The ECB also announced Thursday that it will switch its main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a variable-rate system after June 28 - meaning banks will have to specify a rate they are willing to pay for liquidity. A minimum bid rate of 4.25% has been set for these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Before the switch to the variable-rate system, the ECB said its main refinancing rate for the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  settling June 15 and June 21 also will be set at 4.25%, up from 3.75%. \par \par \uc2  The bank insisted that the decision to switch to variable-rate tenders wasn't intended as a change to its monetary-policy stance. It said the switch was in response to what it called the "severe overbidding" that has developed in the context of the current fixed-rate tender procedure. \par \par \uc2  It added that the change "does not in any way rule out the option that, in the future, the main refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  (of the ECB) may be conducted in principle as fixed-rate tenders." \par \par \uc2  The ECB also raised its marginal lending rate to 5.25% from 4.75%, and the deposit rate to 3.25% from 2.75%, both effective June 9. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's half-point rate increase marks the fifth increase since the ECB took control of euro-zone monetary policy last year. The ECB began its tightening cycle in November and has raised rates by a total of 1.75 percentage points since then. The euro has lost about 17% of its value against the dollar since its launch, and higher rates can make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to investors. \par \par \uc2  Following the ECB's decision, the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key securities lending rate by 0.60 percentage point to 4.70%, but the Swiss National Bank left its three-month Libor target range unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar's gains against the yen followed heavy declines against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days. The yen had been strengthening ahead of a report, due Friday, on Japan's first-quarter gross domestic product. Following data on stronger-than-expected capital expenditures by Japanese corporations released Wednesday, analysts have upwardly revised their forecasts for Japanese GDP growth to an average of 2.8%, compared with a contraction of 1.4% the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  David Ethridge, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysis at Standard & Poor's in New York, predicted that the dollar would continue to trade in a wide range between 104 and 109 yen following the data, with Japanese exporters protecting the top and fear of a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offering support at the bottom of that range. \par \par \uc2  Separately, the Mexican peso continued its slump against the dollar, falling for the third straight day and flirting with the 10-peso per dollar mark on continuing worries about the close contest in the Mexican presidential race. The peso ended at 9.9450 pesos per dollar, weaker than its close Wednesday of 9.8400 to the dollar. Thursday's closing level was the peso's weakest since March 3, 1999. At its worst, the peso fell to 9.98 to the dollar. It last closed above 10 pesos to the dollar on Feb. 10, 1999. \par \par \uc2  (Compiled from Dow Jones Newswires and other sources) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw6801c3e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
