\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar flounders at 7-week low vs euro, yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2000\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 7 (Reuters) - The dollar stumbled to seven-week lows against the euro, the yen and the Swiss franc for the second straight day on Wednesday, undermined by an unfavourable interest rate outlook for the United States. \par \par \uc2  Analysts forecast a rise in euro-zone interest rates as early as Thursday and see an end to Japan's zero interest-rate policy this year. \par \par \uc2  But recent U.S. data have dampened expectations of aggressive monetary tightening by the U.S. Federal Reserve, analysts said, making the dollar look less attractive compared with other other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The key is the marked shift in attitude on the U.S. and the feeling is the Fed may not have to tighten too much, and that is driving the dollar down, not just against the euro but also against the yen and sterling," said David Coleman, chief economist at CIBC World Markets. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose just above $0.96 to its highest since mid-April, before drifting back to around $0.9550 by 1130 GMT. Traders said talk of commercial orders by the Bundesbank kept the euro supported at those levels. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell close to 105 yen and 1.6350 Swiss francs before recovering to around 105.70 and 1.6410 by 1130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also approached four-week lows against sterling set on Tuesday, but found some support after the Bank of England left interest rates unchanged at 6.0 percent, as expected. \par \par \uc2  Euro/sterling , meanwhile, hovered close to five-month highs of 63.00 pence set on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "No change had been pretty much expected by most participants in the market, so it would have been largely priced in," said Geraldine Concagh, economist at AIB Group Treasury in Dublin. "It will provide further support for the euro against sterling as the BOE has done nothing and the ECB is expected to raise rates by at least a quarter point." \par \par \uc2  Comments from Bank of Finland Governor Matti Vanhala that euro area interest rates were "quite low," appeared to back up the view the ECB would raise rates on Thursday, analysts said. "The market is very much expecting an ECB rate hike," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN Amro Bank. "Euro/dollar has come a long way and sentiment is still positive." \par \par \uc2  The euro is up about 9.0 percent from record lows around $0.8840 set on May 19. \par \par \uc2  The euro was little changed after data showing a strong monthly rise of 2.5 percent in German manufacturing orders. \par \par \uc2  Euro-zone retail sales fell 0.3 percent year-on-year in March, the first decline since 1996. \par \par \uc2  YEN EYES \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The yen rose to one-week highs against the euro as well as climbing against the dollar after data showing Japanese firms boosted capital spending - the second-biggest component of gross domestic product - by 3.3 percent from a year earlier, the first rise in nine quarters. \par \par \uc2  Fourteen of 23 economists surveyed by Reuters raised their forecasts for Japanese first quarter gross domestic product growth following the data, to a median forecast of 2.9 percent, from the previous 2.1 percent growth forecast. The GDP data will be released at 2350 GMT on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But further yen gains against the dollar were capped by comments from Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa. \par \par \uc2  Speaking within minutes of the dollar falling to a low of 105.05 yen in Europe, Miyazawa told a news conference the market was aware that the 105 yen level was a strong support against the dollar. He also said Japan would sell yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen's industry-crippling strength on April 3, after the dollar had fallen as far as 102.79. But its previous round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on March 15, followed the dollar's fall to lows below 105 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders said large buy orders, possibly related to options positions, also held the dollar above 105. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa's comments echoed comments in Asia by \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Zembei Mizoguchi and Haruhiko Kuroda that appropriate action would be taken in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent an excessive strengthening of the yen, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  But Mizoguchi differed from BOJ governor Masaru Hayami's upbeat view on the Japanese economy, saying the outlook was unclear. \par \par \uc2  "The MOF is worried about the yen," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa. "They are not really saying anything too negative about the economy, they are just concerned that the yen does not appreciate any more." \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets +44 20 7542 6320, carolyn.cohn@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6703ym1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
