\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro rises vs dollar, eyes seven-week peak. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2000\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 7 (Reuters) - The euro held firm on Wednesday eyeing seven-week highs it set against the dollar a day earlier amid mounting expectations that U.S. and euro zone interest rate differential would narrow rather than widen in coming months. \par \par \uc2  The euro has pierced major chart barriers, staging a near nine-percent gains from its record lows, hit on May 19, in the wake of signs that the U.S. economy was cooling down while euro zone data has been showing robust growth in the region. \par \par \uc2  This has triggered speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates by more than the U.S. Federal Reserve this year, with the latest narrowing in the rate gap expected after the ECB policy meeting on Thursday, when its 3.75 percent refi rate was expected to be raised, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Finland Governor Matti Vanhala said on Wednesday that euro area interest rates were "quite low." "The market is very much expecting an ECB rate hike," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN Amro Bank. "Euro/dollar has come a long way and sentiment is still positive. It could go another big figure higher but to go up more than that will be difficult in the short-term because the market is heavily overbought now and short-term speculative traders will be tempted to take some of their profits now." \par \par \uc2  The euro was within a half cent of Tuesday's seven-week peak of $0.9601, up about 9.0 percent from record lows around $0.8840 set on May 19. On Tuesday alone, it rose more than 1.5 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was tracking the buoyant euro and hitting seven-week peaks around 1.6370 CHF=) against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "A further (ECB) hike tomorrow, coupled with a further 50 basis point in tightening through the second half of the year would place the monetary policy on a more neutral footing and confirm narrowing in interest rate speads necessary for a more durable recovery in the euro," said Paul Meggyesi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The two-year U.S.-euroland spreads has widened a few basis points in the last few days but the trend in this spread is to narrow and given that the spread is already near its lowest levels since January 1999, this remains a positive factor for the euro." \par \par \uc2  YEN BULLS DOMINATE \par \par \uc2  The yen was meanwhile holding near its day's highs against the dollar and the euro, underpinned by capital spending data released by the Finance Ministry on Wednesday, which raised expectations of strong first-quarter economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The ministry said firms boosted capital spending - the second-biggest component of gross domestic product (GDP) - by 3.3 percent from a year earlier, the first rise in nine quarters and beating economists' forecasts ranging from a fall of 1.6 percent to a rise of 0.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists' average estimate for first-quarter GDP, to be released on Friday, is a rise of 2.1 percent, a Reuters survey showed. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held within half a yen of its seven-week highs around 105.15 set against the dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/yen was holding near the day's lows around 100.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's bias is definitely on the downside now," said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  "There are plenty of factors that favour the yen and the euro also looks like it's firmly on a recovery path after breaking 96 cents overnight." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the yen should face some resistance at current levels amid wariness of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen's industry-crippling gains. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda reiterated on Wednesday the view that excessive yen strength was undesirable and that Japan will take action on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time if needed. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also played down the prospects for Japan's economy, saying there is no reason for the yen to strengthen given Japan's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  But his comments were countered by upbeat comments from Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, who said improvements in Japan's economy were becoming clearer and welcomed signs of rising capital spending. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6703y5a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
