\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Slips Against Euro, Rises Versus Pound As Interest Rates Influence Moves \b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2000\par 14:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar finished lower against the euro Wednesday, as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke convincingly through 96 cents on expectations that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates. But the dollar rose nearly one-half cent against the pound after the Bank of England elected to leave United Kingdom rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso tumbled for a second straight day, closing at its lowest level in more than a year on jitters about the July presidential election. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday in New York, the euro traded at 96.17 U.S. cents, up from 95.44 U.S. cents late Tuesday. The British pound fetched $1.5221, down from $1.5276 late Tuesday. The dollar was quoted at 105.54 yen, down slightly from 105.80 yen late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Early in Wednesday's session, the Bank of England announced its decision to leave its benchmark lending rate at the current 6%. Despite the fact that the stance was widely expected, the pound weakened. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets Thursday and is expected to raise its key lending rate by one-quarter percentage point to 4%. \par \par \uc2  Patrick Brodie, assistant vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo Bank in New York, cautioned that the ECB meeting could present a "buy the rumor, sell the fact" situation, sending the euro lower on profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  Others disagreed, saying the euro's break above 96 cents Tuesday and Wednesday has prompted renewed talk of a return to parity with the dollar, a level last seen Feb. 24. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell sharply against the yen in earlier European trading, hitting a low of 105.05 yen, only to bounce back after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry hinted at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa suggested that a push by the markets under 105 yen wouldn't be a wise investment decision. Asked if the dollar near 105 yen was worrying in terms of the nation's economic recovery, which could be hampered by a strengthening yen, Miyazawa said cryptically, "the market has understood from before which corner not to turn." \par \par \uc2  The last time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the dollar was April 3, buying dollars at around 103.30 yen to 105.60 yen, according to estimates at the time. The Japananese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at least 15 times in the past 18 months. \par \par \uc2  The yen has been trending higher ahead of expected strong first-quarter gross-domestic-product data due out of Japan Friday. Analysts expect the GDP figure to come in around 2.0% to 2.5% growth after a contraction in the previous quarter. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been losing ground against its major counterparts since weak U.S. data released last week raised the prospect of a slowdown in the U.S. economy and lowered expectations of further rate hikes by the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso closed sharply lower for a second straight day on worries about the July 2 presidential election. Polls show the top contenders, Francisco Labastida of the ruling Institutional Revolutionary Party and Vicente Fox of the National Action Party, in a statistical dead heat. Investors fear a close vote could lead to post-election turbulence and political instability. \par \par \uc2  The peso finished in Mexico City at 9.84 pesos to the dollar, weaker than Tuesday's close of 9.71 per dollar. Wednesday's close is the weakest since March 5, 1999, when the peso ended at 9.8880 per dollar. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated 3% since last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee ended at a record low against the dollar on sustained corporate dollar demand and lack of supply, dealers said. The dollar ended Wednesday at 44.74 rupees, the Indian unit's lowest closing level ever. \par \par \uc2  (Compiled from Dow Jones Newswires and other sources) \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw6701bfw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
