\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Drops Against All Major Counterparts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Barrett \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2000\par 21:25\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar's days of domination seemed to be dwindling Tuesday, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again endured a significant slide against major counterparts. \par \par \uc2  It slipped a cent-and-a-half against the euro and the pound, and gave up more than two yen and nearly three Swiss francs before recovering somewhat late in the New York session. \par \par \uc2  Profit-taking, rather than a reverse in sentiment, is credited for the partial rebound that lifted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off intraday lows. \par \par \uc2  Analysts blame diminishing expectations of further Fed hikes this month, in the wake of a week's worth of weak U.S. economic data. The dollar has also been dragged down by poorly-performing stocks. Late Tuesday, the Dow Jones industrial average was down about 50 points, while the Nasdaq Composite Index struggled to stay in positive territory, up less than 15 points. \par \par \uc2  The dollar sell-off started in the Asian session and stretched well into New York trading, with the yen the main beneficiary of dollar and, to a lesser degree, euro sales. \par \par \uc2  Late Tuesday, the dollar was trading at Y105.73, sharply lower than Y106.60 early in the New York session and Y107.40 late Monday in New York. The euro was at Y100.98, down from Y101.52 early in the session and from Y101.85 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro had slipped back to $0.9551 late afternoon, up from $0.9537 early in the session and from $0.9483 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro managed to resurface above 96 cents briefly mid-morning, but skidded back more than a half-cent within a quarter-hour and spent the rest of the session struggling to stay in the mid 95-cent range. Still, that's a half-dozen cents more than it was buying just a couple weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  "We've certainly got a very strong move up," said Andy Chaveriat, a technical \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst for BNP Paribas Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro remained short Tuesday of its mid-April high of $0.9670, though it did manage a move just above 96 cents. If the euro climbs above the level in upcoming sessions it would have fully retraced its losses of the past three weeks. A move above the March high of $0.9785 would be more significant, signaling perhaps the start of a longer-term trend to parity at $1.000 or above, said Chaveriat. \par \par \uc2  Despite the euro's backtracking late Tuesday, strategists say Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has strong upside potential ahead of Thursday's meeting of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as expectations rise for a euro-zone rate hike. \par \par \uc2  It's no longer a question of if the ECB will raise rates, but whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost the euro-zone region's 3.75% interest rate by 25 or 50 basis points, according to Michael McGuinness, senior director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at American Express in New York. \par \par \uc2  He added that the market is starting to lean toward a half-percentage-point hike. As long as the ECB raises refinancing rates at least 25 basis points, the euro should continue to climb through the end of the week. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also seems to be benefiting from speculation surrounding a possible increase in the U.K. base borrowing rate. The Bank of England's monetary policy committee's two-day meeting ends Wednesday. While a 25 basis-point rate hike is possible, most market players are expecting the MPC to leave the country's 6.00% base interest rate unchanged following a string of soft data out of the U.K. \par \par \uc2  Sterling climbed to $1.5310 midway through the afternoon, a nearly two-cent jump from its intraday low. Late Tuesday, it was trading at $1.5289, up more than a cent from $1.5189 a day earlier in New York. Against the euro, sterling touched a five-month low near 63 pence before the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retreated about a half-pence to 0.6244 pence late in New York. \par \par \uc2  The yen also made sharp gains against the dollar, in part from short-term investors covering short positions triggered as the dollar skidded through strong technical support levels in the low Y106 area. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Still, analysts say the dollar is not likely to slip below Y105,\par despite its slide to an intraday low of Y105.23 Tuesday, as fears\par persist of a Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at or below that level.\par  -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4384\par   email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dw6600e5o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
