\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW: JP Morgan Asia: Japan Jan-Mar GDP Up 2.3% Q/Q \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2000\par 10:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's economy likely grew by 2.3% in the January-March quarter from the previous quarter, or 9.5% on an annualized, seasonally adjusted basis, J.P. Morgan Securities Asia's chief Japan economist said Friday. \par \par \uc2  A former senior official at the Bank of Japan, Masaaki Kanno said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires that he thinks the nation's gross domestic product grew in the first quarter mainly because of strong exports and healthy public works activity. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese exports were robust and the government's public works projects were good," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  The government is scheduled to release GDP for the first quarter of 2000 at 2350 GMT on June 8, which is 8:50 a.m. June 9 in Tokyo. Consensus estimates call for a 2.4% rise on quarter. \par \par \uc2  Kanno noted that the first quarter rise in GDP will reflect a "payback" for the weak GDP growth figures in the third and fourth quarters of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Japan's GDP shrank by 1.4% on-quarter in the October-December quarter and contracted by 1.0% on-quarter in the June-September quarter last year. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether the government would be able to achieve its projected 0.6% GDP growth target for last fiscal year ended March 31, Kanno said, "I think (the nation's fiscal 1999 GDP growth) will fall a little bit short of that target, but it is likely to be within a margin of error." \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, Kanno predicts the nation's economy will grow around 0.6% on-quarter in the April-June period, or 2.5% on an annualized basis, mainly driven by a strong corporate recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The financial results of Japanese corporates for last fiscal year have been much better than expected," Kanno said, citing a recent Toyo Keizai survey. \par \par \uc2  "According to this survey, corporate revenues rose 17.5% on-year on a non-consolidated basis and jumped 52.1% on-year on a consolidated basis last fiscal year," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  For this fiscal year, the same survey showed that Japanese firms forecast their revenues to rise 15.3% on-year on a non-consolidated basis and to increase 27.5% on-year on a consolidated basis, he added. \par \par \uc2  "The very positive corporate earnings is the biggest contributing factor. Another factor is capital expenditures as reflected by machinery orders, which I believe will continue to be strong in the April-June quarter," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  A third factor is an expected increase in labor demand as corporations step up their capital investment activities, Kanno added. \par \par \uc2  "The labor market has improved a lot if you look at the employment data, such as the latest jobless rate which fell to 4.8% from 4.9% and the rise in the job-offers-to-seekers ratio," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  Kanno added that personal incomes will show "gradual, modest" improvement this year. \par \par \uc2  "I'm estimating a 1% rise in total cash earnings (for individuals) this year from last year and a 1% increase in summer bonuses," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  However, there are lingering doubts whether such an improvement in personal incomes will lead to a rise in consumer demand, Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  "It is still unknown if personal consumption will get better in the near future," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  He pointed out that the recent falls of the Japanese stock markets, uncertainty about Japan's political situation, and concerns about the health of financial institutions could have a negative psychological impact on consumer sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Turning to Bank of Japan's monetary policy, Kanno sees the September-December period as the earliest opportunity for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to abandon its zero interest-rate policy. \par \par \uc2  "The timing for this depends on the status of personal consumption," Kanno said. "If bonuses and consumption recover in summer, I think the BOJ could lift this (zero rate) policy," he added. \par \par \uc2  Consumption-related data due out in late August and early September are likely to be the first "window of opportunity" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change its current monetary policy, he said. \par \par \uc2  Should the BOJ decide to end its zero interest rate policy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to return to targeting the overnight call rate at the 0.25% level that it had before it adopted the zero rate policy, Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the current trend of the yen against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kanno said that he foresees the dollar moving in a core range of Y105.00 to Y110.00 against the yen this year with an upward bias. \par \par \uc2  "There is slim likelihood for the dollar to drop below Y105 this year, given potentially unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ, a widening \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. interest-rate gap, and a reduction in overseas investors' purchases of Japanese equities," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, he foresees more upside risk for the dollar against the yen as a scenario for a soft-landing for the U.S. economy is very likely. \par \par \uc2  "There is a higher possibility for the U.S. economy to make a soft-landing. In such a case, the U.S. stock markets would stabilize, and overseas investors would bring their money into the U.S.," Kanno said. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw6202ql7\par }\page {\par\fs2