\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Withstands Euro Rally Attempt; Pound Hits New Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \uc2 2,023 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2000\par 21:10\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar is slightly lower against the yen and little changed versus the euro late Tuesday in New York, having held off a rally by the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's recent decline against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of two consecutive days of stock sell-offs was capped by somewhat  better performance for U.S. equities. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's rally was very much stock-market induced, and today we're seeing nothing like that," said Tomas Jelf, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg LLC in Stamford, Conn. \par \par \uc2  In fact, after gaining half a cent Monday, the pound fell to a new six-year low of $1.4725 against the dollar Tuesday after members of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee told Parliament that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still at an unsustainable level, especially against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Charles Goodhart, a retiring member of the MPC, said that the recent weakening of sterling against the dollar was beneficial. Mervyn King, deputy governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said sterling could fall "sharply lower" and that the MPC would prefer a weaker pound. \par \par \uc2  Due in part to sterling cross-selling, the euro reached its highest level against the dollar in over a week, testing $0.9150 ahead of the New York equities open. \par \par \uc2  However, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average barely in negative territory Tuesday afternoon and the Nasdaq Composite Index down just 30 points, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't have enough support to keep its gains, falling over a cent over the New York session. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also held its ground against the yen, which has been suffering from weakness in the Nikkei 225. The Japanese stock market shed another 0.4% Tuesday, falling to a new low on the year. \par \par \uc2  However, the yen was supported by comments by Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya hinting at the possibility of putting together a supplemental budget later this year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts see dollar/yen staying rangebound, with talk of a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  below Y106 providing support. \par \par \uc2  Late afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y106.69, down slightly from Y106.72 earlier in the day and lower than Y106.99 Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9034, down sharply from $0.9108 at the open of the session but still higher than $0.9027 late Monday. It was also buying Y96.38, down from Y97.18 earlier Tuesday and Y96.58 a day ago. \par \par \uc2  The pound was at $1.4740, down from $1.4789 in London and $1.4936 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also higher against the Swiss franc, trading at CHF1.7305, up from CHF1.7163 earlier and CHF1.7205 Monday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the dollar's recovery, however, its recent weakness has raised questions about whether the era of the strong dollar is reaching a turning point as the Fed puts the brakes on the economy. \par \par \uc2  "For the first time, the market truly has Fed fears," said Jelf. "It took a while, but now it does." \par \par \uc2  He said that U.S. equity weakness and the ballooning trade balance has "made people cautious about going long dollars. The risk scenario is worse now than it was a few weeks back." \par \par \uc2  However, Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, N.Y., said the interest rate differential between the U.S. and the rest of the world will continue to support the dollar in the near term. \par \par \uc2  "I think this thing about the stock market driving the dollar cheaper is a pile of beans," said Weinberg, adding that, except for the Nasdaq, U.S. stocks are still performing better than European equities because of the weak euro. In euro terms, the Dow Jones Industrial Average is up 1.5% and the Standard & Poor's index is up 5.1%, compared with declines of 12% on London's FTSE index and 0.5% on the German DAX, he said. \par \par \uc2  "You can't just look at the Nasdaq in isolation. If you're living in euroland, the two best performing stock markets in the world are the S&P and Dow," he said. \par \par \uc2  However, Weinberg said that signs of a slowing economy have led him to believe that the Fed will only raise interest rates another 25 basis points, which will reduce the attractiveness of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "As expectations (of further rate hikes) wear off, we believe interest rate spreads will move less favorably to the dollar and we'll see some consolidation," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the near term, however, the focus on the euro has turned to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting Thursday. \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires survey found that over three-quarters of commercial banks which responded don't expect the ECB to raise its 3.75% refinancing rate Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But 89% of the banks surveyed said they expect further rate hikes before the end of the second quarter. After Thursday, the ECB has two more meetings during this quarter. \par \par \uc2  While a rate hike could narrow the euro zone's interest rate differential with the U.S., it still wouldn't be able to match the 6.5% Fed funds rate and may run the risk of making the ECB seem as if it were simply following the Fed. That strategy failed for the central banks of Canada and New Zealand, who saw their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drop after matching the Fed's half-percent rate hike last week. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg's Jelf said he thinks the "market would sell on a premature rate hike because it would be bad for European growth." \par \par \uc2  However, Weinberg said that if the ECB doesn't raise rates, "the euro will take a turn for the worse." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jelf said the euro will likely stay rangebound ahead of the meeting,\par since "the market is certainly not willing to go one way on" either the\par likely outcome or its implications.\par    -By Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385;\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par -0- lr Steady Vs Yen, Euro; Pound Hits New Low\par    By Tom Barkley\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is slightly lower against the yen and\par little changed versus the euro late Tuesday in New York, having held off\par a rally by the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar's recent decline against European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the back of\par two consecutive days of stock sell-offs was capped by somewhat  better\par performance for U.S. equities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Yesterday's rally was very much stock-market induced, and today we're\par seeing nothing like that," said Tomas Jelf, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS\par Warburg LLC in Stamford, Conn.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In fact, after gaining half a cent Monday, the pound fell to a new\par six-year low of $1.4725 against the dollar Tuesday after members of the\par Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee told Parliament that the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was still at an unsustainable level, especially against the\par euro.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Charles Goodhart, a retiring member of the MPC, said that the recent\par weakening of sterling against the dollar was beneficial. Mervyn King,\par deputy governor of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said sterling could fall "sharply\par lower" and that the MPC would prefer a weaker pound.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Due in part to sterling cross-selling, the euro reached its highest\par level against the dollar in over a week, testing $0.9150 ahead of the\par New York equities open.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average barely in negative\par territory Tuesday afternoon and the Nasdaq Composite Index down just 30\par points, the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  didn't have enough support to keep its\par gains, falling over a cent over the New York session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar also held its ground against the yen, which has been\par suffering from weakness in the Nikkei 225. The Japanese stock market\par shed another 0.4% Tuesday, falling to a new low on the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, the yen was supported by comments by Economic Planning Agency\par chief Taichi Sakaiya hinting at the possibility of putting together a\par supplemental budget later this year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Analysts see dollar/yen staying rangebound, with talk of a Bank of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  below Y106 providing support.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y106.69, down slightly\par from Y106.72 earlier in the day and lower than Y106.99 Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro was at $0.9034, down sharply from $0.9108 at the open of the\par session but still higher than $0.9027 late Monday. It was also buying\par Y96.38, down from Y97.18 earlier Tuesday and Y96.58 a day ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pound was at $1.4740, down from $1.4789 in London and $1.4936 the\par day before.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was also higher against the Swiss franc, trading at\par CHF1.7305, up from CHF1.7163 earlier and CHF1.7205 Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the dollar's recovery, however, its recent weakness has raised\par questions about whether the era of the strong dollar is reaching a\par turning point as the Fed puts the brakes on the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the first time, the market truly has Fed fears," said Jelf. "It\par took a while, but now it does."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said that U.S. equity weakness and the ballooning trade balance has\par "made people cautious about going long dollars. The risk scenario is\par worse now than it was a few weeks back."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Carl Weinberg, chief economist at High Frequency Economics in\par Valhalla, N.Y., said the interest rate differential between the U.S. and\par the rest of the world will continue to support the dollar in the near\par term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think this thing about the stock market driving the dollar cheaper\par is a pile of beans," said Weinberg, adding that, except for the Nasdaq,\par U.S. stocks are still performing better than European equities because\par of the weak euro. In euro terms, the Dow Jones Industrial Average is up\par 1.5% and the Standard & Poor's index is up 5.1%, compared with declines\par of 12% on London's FTSE index and 0.5% on the German DAX, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You can't just look at the Nasdaq in isolation. If you're living in\par euroland, the two best performing stock markets in the world are the S&P\par and Dow," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Weinberg said that signs of a slowing economy have led him to\par believe that the Fed will only raise interest rates another 25 basis\par points, which will reduce the attractiveness of higher U.S. interest\par rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "As expectations (of further rate hikes) wear off, we believe interest\par rate spreads will move less favorably to the dollar and we'll see some\par consolidation," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the near term, however, the focus on the euro has turned to the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Dow Jones Newswires survey found that over three-quarters of\par commercial banks which responded don't expect the ECB to raise its 3.75%\par refinancing rate Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But 89% of the banks surveyed said they expect further rate hikes\par before the end of the second quarter. After Thursday, the ECB has two\par more meetings during this quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While a rate hike could narrow the euro zone's interest rate\par differential with the U.S., it still wouldn't be able to match the 6.5%\par Fed funds rate and may run the risk of making the ECB seem as if it were\par simply following the Fed. That strategy failed for the central banks of\par Canada and New Zealand, who saw their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  drop after matching the\par Fed's half-percent rate hike last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par UBS Warburg's Jelf said he thinks the "market would sell on a\par premature rate hike because it would be bad for European growth."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Weinberg said that if the ECB doesn't raise rates, "the euro\par will take a turn for the worse."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jelf said the euro will likely stay rangebound ahead of the meeting,\par since "the market is certainly not willing to go one way on" either the\par likely outcome or its implications.\par    -By Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385;\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \pa