\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Dollar To Gain On Euro, But Struggle Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Barrett \par \uc2 1,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2000\par 00:13\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- In the wake of the half-point hike in the U.S. benchmark borrowing rates last week, and the euro's ensuing two-cent slide, the market is keeping a close eye on euro-zone central bankers this week to see what - if anything - they can do to prop up the struggling single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro nearly set a new all-time low against the dollar Friday, falling to $0.8850 before bouncing back more than a cent on sharp losses in the U.S. stock market. But with little else to keep Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up, analysts widely expect the euro to retest the all-time low of $0.8845 in the upcoming week. \par \par \uc2  "This is the result of the lack of decisive action on the part of the Europeans and the reluctance of our own Fed to get on the bandwagon to support the euro," said John Schein, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Generale Bank in New York, referring to the euro's brush with its all-time low against the dollar Friday morning. \par \par \uc2  "It's exactly what we were expecting. It was just a matter of time," Said Schein. \par \par \uc2  By late Friday, the euro had recovered to trade at $0.8991, up from $0.8877 early in the New York session and $0.8937 late Thursday in New York. Traders attributed the euro's one-and-a-half cent rebound during Friday's New York session to the slide in U.S. stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Rumors of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the euro have also helped prop up the sickly single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But speculation has subsided as euro-zone central bankers and other officials continually downplayed the possibility while it became increasingly apparent that a joint coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the U.S. and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely. \par \par \uc2  The ECB meets Thursday to consider raising refinancing rates in the euro-zone region. Few believe the bank could justify anything more than a 25 basis-point increase, as growth in the U.S. far outpaces that in the euro zone, and European central bankers don't want to hinder economic growth in their 11 member countries. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision may hinge on inflation data due out Wednesday for France and Germany, the largest economies in the euro-zone region. The French consumer price index and German producer price index for April, as well as Italian and U.K. trade data for March and April, are slated for release Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The pound has slid steadily since the Fed raised rates last Tuesday, as expectations for matching rate hike in the U.K. diminished. On Friday, sterling slid to a new six-year low against the dollar of $1.4751. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee doesn't meet for another two weeks, but few expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise the U.K.'s base borrowing rate when it meets after it opted to leave rates alone at 6.0% at the last meeting. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the pound was trading at $1.4886. \par \par \uc2  "The U.K. economy has been slowing down and indications are that they aren't getting the inflation they thought they would, so the monetary policy committee is doing what it's supposed to, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is getting weaker," said John Forman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at IFR \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Watch in Boston. \par \par \uc2  Forman said sterling was so oversold following the Fed decision that it may be in for a bounce up in the upcoming week. "But I'm afraid that even if it does, there's a good chance it will roll back over again," he added. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, even if the ECB raises rates next week, it is not likely to provide much more than a temporary balm to the bruised and battered euro. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hit by a surge in both the dollar and yen last week - dropping Friday to a new low of Y94.91 against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to $0.8850, just short of its lowest level against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Though the euro then staged a remarkable recovery in New York trading Friday against the dollar, regaining more than a cent, it languished within a yen of the new all-time low against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Refinancing rates in the euro zone are now at 3.75% - sharply lower than the 6.50% U.S. Fed funds rate, after the most recent increase. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates in Japan are even lower, but that hasn't kept the yen from strengthening against the dollar - and the euro, in particular - over the past week. The dollar gained against every widely-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed rate increase except the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , against which it gave up more than two yen by week's end. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the dollar was at Y106.90, down more than a yen from Y107.64 early in New York trading, and more than a 2-yen drop from Y108.57 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar dropped as low as Y106.37 Friday during a dismal day in the U.S. stock market, in a yen-buying frenzy that began during the Asian session after comments from Japanese finance officials reinforcing expectations the country's economy is on its way to recovery. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami indicated Friday that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will "normalize" - or lift - its zero-interest-rate policy once it's sure the economy is improving. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa also reiterated his upbeat assessment of the country's economic prospects during the regularly scheduled news conference. \par \par \uc2  Economic data out of Tokyo in the week ahead could provide confirmation the bank and the market will be watching for. If a March service-industry index released Monday comes in as high as expected, first quarter growth estimates could be in for an upward revision, further boosting mainstream investor sentiment toward Japan. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar's steep decline Friday, analysts say it may be poised for further losses against the yen in the upcoming week. \par \par \uc2  Still, strong support at the Y106-mark will likely stem any dollar losses in the week ahead. \par \par \uc2  "There hasn't been a heck of a lot of damage done," said Forman, who believes the bias is still to the upside over the upcoming week, "but only marginally so." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan hosted a meeting of the Deputy Finance Ministers of the Group of\par Seven leading industrialized countries over weekend, a high-ranking G7\par finance ministry official told Dow Jones Newswires Friday. However, the\par deputies meeting in Kyoto weren't expected to discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par focusing instead on Information Technology issues.\par -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4384\par  email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010805dw5l003ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
