\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro trapped in tight ranges ahead of FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabrina Ghani \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2000\par 09:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates prices with fresh quotes, changes byline and dateline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 16 (Reuters) - The euro retained a soft tone against the dollar and yen on Tuesday amid waning fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although trading was confined to narrow ranges ahead of a U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  The euro was undermined after Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke was quoted by German business daily Handelsblatt as saying he did not expect the big \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  blocs - Europe, the United States and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to stage concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the euro in coming months. \par \par \uc2  But traders said the euro was supported by wariness ahead of Federal Open Market Committee meeting later in the day. There was a risk U.S. equity markets might sell off on the Fed's rate decision or accompanying statement and drag the dollar down. \par \par \uc2  According to Reuters' poll on Friday, 27 of 29 primary dealers expected a half point increase in Fed fund rates to 6.50. \par \par \uc2  "Welteke's comments echoed what we have been saying, but it may have disappointed people who had bought the euro on the misguided perception that they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Cameron Crise, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Warburg Dillon Read in London. \par \par \uc2  "If we get a 50 basis point rate hike, it would be no shakes and I don't think there would be a huge fall out in the dollar or U.S. assets, unless there is a frightening statement accompanying it. If we get a 25 basis point rate hike, it'll be mayhem and asset markets will dive." \par \par \uc2  The euro was hovering near $0.91 in Europe after slipping to $0.9069, down about a cent from last week's peak of $0.92. Euro/yen slipped about a yen on the day to trough at 98.78 yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders said in light of Welteke's comments, the euro was looking vulnerable and a break of $0.9000/30 could turn sentiment bearish. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the comments were very wise or helpful and I don't know whether they were sanctioned by other members of the ECB," said Marian Bell, treasury economist at Royal Bank of Scotland. \par \par \uc2  "To rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage is not very helpful given it is one of the things the ECB could do if the euro started falling again." \par \par \uc2  YEN EDGES UP AFTER HAYAMI COMMENTS \par \par \uc2  The yen was bolstered by comments from Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami who reiterated earlier in Asia that the BOJ could end its zero rate policy when personal incomes clearly stopped falling. \par \par \uc2  "Hayami's comments spurred some stop-loss selling, but the amounts were not large. The moves were exaggerated by thin volumes," said Hideyuki Tsukamoto, manager for Fuji Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stabilised around 109.00 in Europe after slipping to 108.78 yen in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, traders in Tokyo said news that Japanese Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa had been accused by a lawmaker of paying him off when he was prime minister in 1991 was having only a limited impact on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Going forward, dealers said the dollar might garner some support due to yen-selling interest among foreign operators. \par \par \uc2  "The market's views on the outlook for Japanese shares has turned a bit bearish," said Takayuki Togawa, manager for Tokai Bank Ltd in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Such cautious views may spur more fund repatriation by overseas operators, which was behind the dollar's rise above 110 yen last week, Togawa said. \par \par \uc2  "The Nikkei 225 average's dollar-based performance has been poor recently, fuelling talk that a second and third wave of yen-selling by overseas operators may emerge," Togawa said. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 closed up 237.56 points at 17,551.25. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5g00gzv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
