\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Kuroda: Concerned That Euro Is "Quite Weak" \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Simms \par \uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI -(Dow Jones)- Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda said he is concerned that the euro is "quite weak" and that its level doesn't reflect the euro-zone's strong economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also worried about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness, Kuroda told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. But he added that he doesn't know what kind of action the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take. \par \par \uc2  "The basic concern I share is that the euro has been quite weak, which doesn't reflect the relatively strong fundamentals of the euro area," Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  "Not in the last several days, but in the last several months, the euro has been weak and declining despite the fact that the euro area economy has been recovering quite strongly," he said. \par \par \uc2  He declined to comment on whether joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the ECB, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. to support the euro might occur. \par \par \uc2  Regarding the yen, Kuroda repeated that the most recent Group of Seven communique says that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  need to reflect economic fundamentals and that the yen therefore must parallel the delicate state of the nation's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Although the economy has "bottomed out," economic data are still mixed overall, with private capital investment increasing but consumption weak, Kuroda said. \par \par \uc2  He said he wasn't worried about the possibility of the yen shooting up on expected strong Japanese January-March gross domestic product data due out in early June. Some analysts say that GDP could jump by 10% on an annualized basis, and that might put upward pressure on the yen. \par \par \uc2  "Market people have become more knowledgeable about the quarterly fluctuations of the economy and I don't think the market would be misguided by big growth rate figures in one quarter after the experience in 1999. I have no concern about an overreaction in the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  In June of 1999, the yen surged against the dollar after the release of surprisingly good GDP data for the January-March quarter which showed the economy had expanded at an annual pace of 7.9%. The yen rise forced Japanese monetary authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times during the month, selling between $15 to $20 billion worth of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said that even if GDP expanded by 7% to 8% on an annualized basis in the first quarter, this would be coming off of negative growth in the October-December period, and that such a fast pace of growth would be unsustainable in coming quarters. He said that he expected 1% growth in the current fiscal year started April 1. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kuroda is in Shanghai to attend a conference on China and Asia\par sponsored by the Asia Society and Dow Jones & Co.\par    -James Simms, (86-21) 6218-3268; james.simms@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02c7x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
