\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: BOJ Urging MOF To Reveal  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Data \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kenneth McCallum \par \uc2 1,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 02:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Some Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are urging their counterparts at the Finance Ministry to consider releasing data about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the public, a move that would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to join the ranks of leading industrialized nations that already announce such information. \par \par \uc2  BOJ and MOF officials are holding "informal and confidential" talks about the issue, says a monetary source. \par \par \uc2  The outcome is still far from certain. Even if the MOF ultimately agrees to release more information about Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's unclear how much detail it would provide. It might only say how much money was spent during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a few months after the fact. \par \par \uc2  But any decision to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  information by the MOF, which is in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, would be a big step by Japan toward meeting the global standard on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities of all of the other industrialized nations, including the Federal Reserve, already have a policy of disclosing some form of data about the scale of their market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan has lagged its world partners in this regard. \par \par \uc2  Part of the reason for this is that Japanese officials consider the details of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a national secret, and almost never even admit that the BOJ enters the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from time to time. \par \par \uc2  MOF officials seem averse to using the word "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " at all. In rare instances when officials actually declare that they entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, their preferred phrase is that they "took appropriate action" in the market. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Public Not Completely In The Dark About \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The public isn't completely in the dark as to how much money changes\par hands during \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . They can look at monthly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserve figures, which spike up when the BOJ sells large amounts of yen\par in the market. In June of 1999, for example, when the MOF was\par desperately trying to keep the dollar above Y120, reserves jumped by\par $22.718 billion to $246.377 billion. In a typical month, reserves\par usually increase by a little more than $1 billion, due to investment\par returns on foreign securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some BOJ officials feel that the reserves data aren't good enough. If\par analysts only have such data at their disposal, they have to guess the\par amount of money spent in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , meaning it's impossible for them\par to come up with a truly accurate reading on how effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par was. The officials feel that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should make itself more accountable\par to the public by allowing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data to be studied.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In the past, when members of parliament asked Finance Ministry\par officials to release information about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the officials would\par refuse, saying that the information is a state secret," says the\par monetary source. "But since we are using taxpayers' money to conduct\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , shouldn't we reveal to the public whether or not the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are effective?"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the MOF makes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data available for public perusal, the\par figures might support the view held by a number of officials in the BOJ:\par that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only has a temporary effect on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, and\par sometimes it's even counterproductive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOJ officials Takeshi Kimura and Kou Nakayama make this point\par indirectly in a recent research paper. In it, the two argue that the yen\par tends to strengthen more rapidly when it's volatile. And yen-selling\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has tended to cause the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to become more\par volatile.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In other words, yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has often caused the yen to\par ultimately rise even more sharply. The two never come right out and say\par this, but that's what they really want to say, a source says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Divergent Views On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Probably no BOJ official would oppose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop rapid moves\par in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. But there do seem to be differences in the way the\par two sides look at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Contrast the BOJ and MOF's recent rhetoric about the yen. In its April\par monthly economic report, the BOJ took out its observation that the\par stronger yen would hurt corporate profits, something the report had been\par repeating for many months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, two of the MOF's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials, Haruhiko Kuroda\par and Zembei Mizoguchi, continue to insist that a strong yen is bad for\par the economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The divergent views may reflect the kinds of people that the two\par institutions serve. The BOJ, as an independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , can afford\par to look at the overall effect of the rising yen. While a rapid yen\par appreciation could hurt the economy, a strong yen isn't completely bad:\par it improves the bottom line of importers and makes Japanese financial\par assets more attractive to foreigners, among other things.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MOF, on the other hand, must deal extensively with politicians\par because it's also responsible for the national budget and taxes. One\par view is that smaller Japanese companies, which form a big voting bloc\par for the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, lobby politicians to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par when the yen strengthens. That's because a yen rise might hurt the\par profitability of blue-chip exporters, from which the smaller companies\par get their business. The politicians in turn put pressure on the MOF to\par take action in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. One official calls this arrangement\par "a form of moral hazard."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some sources say that the MOF doesn't dismiss out of hand the idea of\par revealing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data, and is willing to discuss it with the BOJ.\par But other officials, even those in the BOJ, appear to doubt that the MOF\par would agree to go through with such a step.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par One government official says the MOF might agree to release\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data if the figures are released "a long time after" the\par actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But the official added that the MOF would probably be\par reluctant to reveal too much about the way it conducts \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par because that might make \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  less of a surprise and therefore\par less effective.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The surprise value is one weapon" to make \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effective, the\par official says. "Why would they want to get rid of a good weapon from\par their arsenal?"\par -By Kenneth McCallum; 813-5255-2938; ken.mccallum@dowjones.com\par -0- 12/05/00 00-01G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5c02c0e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
