\par\par \b \uc2  Central Banks' Shifting Loyalties: From Tsys To Agencies \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry \par \uc2 1,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2000\par 21:15\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The world's central banks, who have always been among the biggest and most loyal holders of U.S. Treasury notes and bonds, are starting to look elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  While they are keeping their giant portfolio of investments in the U.S., many of these central banks are gaining a taste for "agencies," the debt issued by government-sponsored enterprises, or GSEs. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, these cash-rich institutions continue to keep a lot of money in Treasurys. Indeed, there are good reasons for them to do so - the pool of available Treasurys  is shrinking as the government works to lower its debt burden, raising prices in the process, and recent trends have lifted Treasury yields to potentially attractive levels. At more than 6.5%, yields on the ten-year note are well above the 2% offered on equivalent Japanese government  bonds. \par \par \uc2  These short-term attractions notwithstanding, central banks are losing their single-minded focus on Treasurys. That's largely because agencies offer a better return on their money. \par \par \uc2  "The trend over the last three years clearly has been for our foreign official and international account customers to purchase more GSEs," a New York Federal Reserve official said. \par \par \uc2  The New York Fed acts as custodian for foreign central banks and other foreign official institutions including the  World Bank and the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  As of Wednesday, the Fed held in custody $705 billion of Treasury and agency securities on behalf of these foreign institutions, of which $86 billion was agency debt,  the New York Fed said. \par \par \uc2  That's double the $42 billion in agency debt these institutions held via the Fed at the end of 1997, when total Treasury and agency debt was some $670 billion, according to the New York Fed. \par \par \uc2  Mike Ryan, managing director and senior investment strategist at PaineWebber in New York, says there are initial signs that central banks "have now begun to move away from the Treasury market and into (higher yielding) agency debt, corporate debt and mortgage-backed securities" in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Many foreign central banks "are under pressure from their governments to increase the returns on their reserve holdings," Ryan says. "It's no longer a case of: 'buy gold, buy Treasurys and go home", he said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par        Volatile Ride, So Far\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They haven't been entirely happy with their forays away  from\par Treasurys, however. Remarks by U.S. government officials that raised\par questions about the status of agency debt led to a temporary respite in\par central banks' sally into non-Treasury debt in March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "During the last year, foreign central banks have been  active in\par buying agency debt," notes Michael Cheah, a portfolio manager at Sun\par America Asset Management in New York. "But once the issue was raised of\par whether this debt would be guaranteed by the US government, they were\par naturally frightened away. Foreign banks have been pulling out," he\par says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now, with yield spread on agencies over equivalent Treasurys having\par narrowed from their March highs, central banks have returned to the GSE\par debt market, says PaineWebber's Ryan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Agencies are natural place for central banks to go in search of\par returns better than those offered by Treasurys because their internal\par rules prevent them from seeking out corporate debt and other securities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They like to pick up yield and most central banks have fairly strict\par rules to invest in sovereign, or quasi-sovereign debt," notes Henry\par Willmore, chief U.S. Economist at Barclays Capital in New York. Apart\par from Treasurys and U.S. agency debt, there aren't many other low-risk,\par high yielding alternatives for central banks, apart from Germany's\par partly mortgage-backed  "Pfandbrief"  securities, issued by the\par Landesbanken,  Willmore notes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       Reserves  To Invest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, those foreign central banks that chose to raise their\par holdings in euros at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's inception have seen the value of\par these assets plunge. These players may have more confidence in\par dollar-denominated assets at this time and be tempted by the\par comparatively high yields for U.S. bonds, notes one government bond\par strategist.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another effect of the euro's introduction has been to leave individual\par European central banks holding a combined total of some $300 billion\par reserve assets as of November, Ryan estimates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These funds were once used to defend their respective national\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but since the banks have already committed other reserves to\par the ECB for those purposes, they are now available for longer term\par portfolio-type investments.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Similar developments have occurred at the Bank of Japan, for different\par reasons.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ, arguably the most influential non-US \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in world\par markets has seen its dollar reserves grow over the past year as it has\par periodically bought dollars in  an effort to weaken the yen. The BOJ is\par the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's agent for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On April 18, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced new guidelines for managing\par external assets. These separate investments into a "high liquidity"\par portfolio comprised of securities with maturities of one year or less\par and an investment portfolio which "takes returns into (greater)\par consideration," a BOJ official in New York said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked what the BOJ's strategy was in terms of investing  in U.S.\par Treasury securities and U.S. agency debt, the official declined to\par comment.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the start of April, reports appeared in the Japanese  press that\par the Bank of Japan would start buying more medium-term foreign debt as\par well as short-term issues.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The BOJ was said at that time to have snapped up $9 billion in\par Treasury bills and notes, following its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the BOJ's April 18 statement does specify interest in "investments\par in securities and deposits with maturity not exceeding five years,\par denominated in the US dollar, euro and pound sterling."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Wednesday, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adopted new rules aimed at\par increasing the transparency of its external reserves, running to some\par $340 billion. In time, these changes may help shed more light on the\par BOJ'S activity in the agency debt market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By John Parry; Dow Jones Newswires\par    john.parry@dowjones.com; 201-938-2096\par \f28 \par 