\par\par \b \uc2  Japan steps up disclosure on external reserves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Mai 2000\par 12:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 10 (Reuters) - Japan adopted new standards on Wednesday aimed at raising the transparency of its massive external reserves, a move welcomed by economists after years of mystery-shrouded management by the powerful Finance Ministry. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the new disclosure rules should act as a constraint on the ministry's conduct of policy as it would be forced to account for its actions more fully. \par \par \uc2  But they added that still more transparency was needed to get a full picture of how the government manages its $340 billion stockpile, the biggest of any economy. \par \par \uc2  A senior Finance Ministry official told reporters the new standards had been adopted in line with its commitment to the International Monetary Fund's Special Data Dissemination Standard (SDSS), which had also been adopted by 46 other economies. \par \par \uc2  "This is a big step forward. Transparency in external reserves is indispensable as the reserves are the property of Japanese people," said Sayuri Kawamura, senior economist at the economics department of Japan Research Institute Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "In the past, we had to speculate on how the government was managing the reserves, but with the information available now we can easily see how they are managed," Kawamura said. \par \par \uc2  SECRET DOLLAR DEPOSITS UNMASKED \par \par \uc2  Japan's foreign reserves, which hit a record high $338.582 billion at the end of April, are mainly made up of the government's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, gold and IMF reserve positions. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , can use the reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, something it has done several times in recent months to weaken what it sees as an excessively strong yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants said the key change under the new rules was that the ministry would for the first time disclose the exact amount of its so-called "MOF depo" - dollar-denominated term-deposits made at commercial banks. \par \par \uc2  The new reserve data released on Wednesday showed MOF deposits at domestic banks stood at $43.623 billion and those at foreign banks in Japan at $20.471 billion. The ministry, which had previously kept the very existence of such deposits secret, said historical data was unavailable. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the deposits had been an effective way for the ministry to influence banks, working as a cushion when Japanese banks had difficulty in securing enough dollars in the international marketplace. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said that MOF deposits could have reached over $100 billion in late 1997 when the "Japan premium" - an extra funding cost slapped on Japanese banks - shot up in response to a series of failures among financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "This is a step in the right direction but even further disclosure may be necessary such as a breakdown of what \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the MOF is holding," one banker said. \par \par \uc2  "Also we want detailed information on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as why and under what circumstance the MOF decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the banker said. \par \par \uc2  SDDS METHOD PUSHES UP AMOUNT OF OFFICIAL RESERVES \par \par \uc2  Calculating by the MOF's traditional method, the reserves were at $317.148 billion at the end of April, up $11.636 billion from a month earlier. \par \par \uc2  Of the difference of about $21.4 billion between the SDDR-based data and the one based on the old method, $9.1 billion represented book profit on securities and gold held in the official reserves, and the accrued interest on its bond holdings and deposits. \par \par \uc2  The SDDS also introduced the mark-to-market accounting standard for Japan's reserves. For example, the value of its gold reserves rose to $6.664 billion compared to around $1.1 billion in March, the official said. \par \par \uc2  The remaining $12.3 billion represented newly covered items such as redeemable long-term deposits at banks. Those deposits were previously excluded from the reserves as they were less liquid than shorter-maturity deposits. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5a00v5t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
