\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro soft, sentiment hit by Kuehbacher's remarks. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2000\par 14:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 9 (Reuters) - The euro backtracked against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as a senior Bundesbank official's downbeat comments added to bearish sentiment about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prospects. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank council member Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher spurred a flurry of euro selling on Tuesday by saying he saw few signs of sustainable euro strength in the short-term. \par \par \uc2  He added there was no point in the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  without cooperation from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States. His remarks dimmed recent speculation of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to support the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  Although Bundesbank president Ernst Welteke is the only Bundesbank official to have a say in the ECB's decision making process since he sits on the ECB's council, analysts said Kuehbacher's comments hit the market as he is perceived as influential within the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "His remarks are going to be perceived as being indicative of how the Bundesbank itself sees the euro," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic advisor at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  "If you've got the German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively telling the market that's there is no great deal of hope of the euro turning around in the near-term, then people will start looking for it to go the other way." \par \par \uc2  The German state \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Berlin and Brandenburg, which Kuehbacher heads, also put pressure on the euro by saying there was no need to hike further the ECB's refinancing rate, currently at 3.75 percent. The rate was last raised on April 27. \par \par \uc2  By 1145 GMT the euro was hovering near session lows around $0.8950. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had quickly erased about half a cent of its value after Kuehbacher's remarks. But it was still more than a cent away from last week's record lows. \par \par \uc2  The euro also lost ground against the yen after Kuehbacher's remarks, but by 1145 GMT it was retesting 98 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has been dragged down by the recent comments by Kuehbacher," said Lee Ferridge, head global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Rabobank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still lingering in the market, but they have diminished since his comments and will not linger for long without some delivery." \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session a drop in Germany's jobless rate to 9.8 percent, the lowest since 1996, highlighted the fact that the unemployment trend in Europe's biggest economy was improving, providing some support to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  FEARS NOT TOTALLY DISSIPATED \par \par \uc2  The market had punished the euro on Monday as euro zone finance ministers meeting in Brussels said they shared a "common concern" about the present level of the euro but did not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Despite the bland euro-11 official statement, traders said the market was giving European authorities the benefit of the doubt and was waiting to see if any surprise action had been secretly decided at Monday's meeting. \par \par \uc2  Wariness was reinforced by French Finance Minister Laurent Fabius and German Finance Minister Hans Eichel, who both said on Monday that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained an option. \par \par \uc2  With the market already focusing on a ECB monetary policy meeting on Thursday, analysts said fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined with the chance of another rate hike would keep the euro above its record lows until then. \par \par \uc2  "At this stage the euro is getting some support just by speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the ECB board meeting on Thursday," said Hawkins at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "People might be concerned about a double whammy of higher interest rates and ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time. This is proving a cushion to the euro between here and Thursday." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile dollar/yen had firmed to day's highs around 109.20/30, benefiting from the slippage in euro/dollar. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5900sr3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
