\par\par \b \uc2  Euro Recoups Some Losses On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Barrett \par \uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2000\par 21:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)-Growing expectations of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the single  \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the euro gain a cent on the dollar Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It wasn't a case of general dollar weakness, analysts said. Despite a disappointing day on Wall Street, the dollar remained firm against most non-European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,  including the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro remained at least a cent above its all-time low of $0.8845 throughout the New York session. Still, most analysts expect it will soon slide backwards unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps in. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, French officials made a direct appeal to the international community. During a speech in Hungary, French Prime Minister Lionel Jospin demanded concerted international effort to boost the euro, indicating that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide is becoming intolerable. \par \par \uc2  Jean-Claude Trichet, governor of the Bank of France, echoed Jospin's concerns, suggesting that efforts by the U.S., Japan, and other large economies could bring the euro's level up to one which reflects the region's strong fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Bundesbank member Klaus-Dieter Kuehbacher also weighed in, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fail without the cooperation of the U.S. and Japan \par \par \uc2  An unusually large increase in U.S. Federal Reserve Bank deposits fueled further speculation among dealers that such cooperation was already underway, with the ECB "concentrating dollar reserves" for future \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But U.S. officials have given no indication  they plan to participate in a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The market is of the belief that the ECB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be for it,"  said Joe Francomano, a strategic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Erste  Bank in New York. "But the U.S. stance has apparently been that they won't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the ECB." \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, even the prospect of such action was enough to boost the euro. With the ECB meeting Thursday, many analysts expect the euro to continue climbing Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Though analysts don't expect a euro-zone interest rate  hike, "there is some expectation that the ECB will do something to prop up the euro," said Paul Podolsky, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at FleetBoston. \par \par \uc2  Raising interest rates may not be the answer, particularly as the February year-over-year inflation rate  for the euro zone's 11 member countries - the most recent  figure available - was 1.2%, well below the ECB's 2% ceiling. Even if the bank were to raise rates, the euro-zone's current refinancing rates are 3.75% compared to the 6.0% benchmark borrowing rate in the U.S. - a figure expected to grow by as much as 50 basis points next  week when the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee meets. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very tough position the ECB is in," said Doug Porter, an economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns Securities Ltd. in Toronto. "In some respects just the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be their best method." \par \par \uc2  Still, Porter added that most market players expect the ECB to carry through if the euro falls much further. Meanwhile, the euro was trading at $0.9058, up about a cent from $0.8964 early in the New York session and up from $0.8975 late Monday in New York. Against the yen, the  euro was at Y99.16, up a yen from Y98.15 early in New York trading and from Y97.90 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was buying Y109.47, relatively flat from Y109.49 early in the Tuesday's session, but up from Y109.08 late Monday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar remains at its strongest levels against the  yen in about two months, rising during New York trading to within a half-yen of the psychologically and technically significant Y110 level. Unexpectedly weak Japanese March consumer spending data released early Tuesday and a nearly 2% drop in Tokyo's Nikkei 225 stock index put early pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with yen-for-euro sales dragging it down again later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The euro's bounce back Tuesday helped boost other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, with the dollar trading against the Swiss franc late Tuesday at CHF1.7167, down from CHF1.7248 late Monday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The pound was quoted at $1.5328, little changed from $1.5324 late\par Monday.\par    -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4384\par email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020010802dw59009qn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
