\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Usui says monitoring  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  moves carefully. \b0\par\par\uc2 217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2000\par 11:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuters) - Japanese Vice Finance Minister Nobuaki Usui said on Monday he was carefully monitoring movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but declined to comment on the recent rise in the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the dollar's break above 109 yen on Monday, Usui told a regular news conference: "We are always paying attention to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and monitoring them carefully." \par \par \uc2  On the recent plunge in the euro, Usui said he understood euro-zone authorities were concerned about the euro, a sentiment expressed by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg. \par \par \uc2  The ECB chief last week took the unusual step of reassuring EU citizens over the future of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the strength of the European economy, boosting speculation of euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements should reflect economic fundamentals and has expressed concern over the euro's continued slide. \par \par \uc2  The euro was bolstered above last week's record lows against the dollar and yen by early London trade on Monday on speculation that a meeting of European finance ministers in Brussels could release a statement supportive of the euro. \par \par \uc2  ((Yoko Nishikawa, Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw58013py\par