\par\par \b \uc2  Euro steals ahead, bears bothered by Ecofin buzz. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2000\par 08:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, May 8 (Reuters) - The euro crept cautiously higher on Monday as bears fretted that a meeting of European Finance Ministers might produce more than mere rhetoric in its defence. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was abuzz with talk the meeting would produce at least a statement of support and quite possibly an agreement to double the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserve ceiling. \par \par \uc2  While many felt such an outcome would be largely cosmetic, they conceded it would keep alive speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was finally prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. \par \par \uc2  That was enough to squeeze the euro above Friday's $0.9010 high to reach $0.9012 in late trade, giving it some leeway atop last week's lifetime low of $0.8840. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported stiff resistance just ahead at $0.9030 but saw scope to reach $0.9150 given how short the market was. It also edged up to 98.10 yen from 97.29 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar kept its firmer trend against the yen to stand at 108.85 against 108.52 on Friday, but an early push above 109.00 was frustrated by active selling from Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  Attention was riveted on the European Finance Ministers meeting where the euro would most certainly be on the agenda. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said rumour had it the meeting would produce a statement of support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  along the usual lines that its weakness was not warranted by fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  There was also talk ministers would finally approve an ECB request for a doubling of its reserve ceiling to 100 billion euros ($90 billion). The request, made last September, had met opposition and analysts had thought it would not be passed in full. \par \par \uc2  However, with the latest slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  piling on the pressure, enacting the bill offered a relatively painless way to provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with support, if largely psychological. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said such a move would have some "announcement" effect but in reality would be largely cosmetic given the ECB can already raise more funds through swaps with other central banks. \par \par \uc2  "It's not a question of money, they've got truck loads. Actually, the eurozone as a whole has a lot more (foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) reserves than they need," said a European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "It's a question of will. Do they have the will to use it? The market suspects not." \par \par \uc2  The risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to loom large last week when ECB President Wim Duisenberg took the unusual step of reassuring EU citizens that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a hospital case. \par \par \uc2  The theme was picked up by Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke in an interview in Die Welt newspaper on Sunday when he said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could never be ruled out. \par \par \uc2  But, tellingly for traders, Welteke also emphasised that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried great risks and should only be contemplated when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sure of success. \par \par \uc2  And here is the rub. Past experience - as with the dollar in 1995 - shows that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends can only be turned by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States. \par \par \uc2  Analysts believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be only too happy to join in as the yen's rise against the euro was hurting exporters and Japanese investors holding a mountain of euro debt. \par \par \uc2  But circumstances were very different for the U.S. where the strong dollar was exactly what the economy needed. It was helping both to restrain inflation and to attract the foreign capital necessary to fund the country's huge trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts found it hard to see why the Clinton administration, in an election year, would assist in driving the dollar lower when that risked scaring foreign investors out of U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was suffering in comparison with the U.S. economy which continued to amaze with its vitality. \par \par \uc2  The level of confidence there was aptly illustrated by how quickly markets accepted the view that the Federal Reserve should become more aggressive and hike interest rates by 50 basis points at its May 16 meeting. \par \par \uc2  A couple of weeks ago even a whiff of such a move would have scared investors witless, but on Friday strong U.S. jobs data convinced the market that a half-point increase was warranted and U.S. equities rallied as a result. \par \par \uc2  "As long as people have this much confidence in the U.S., the dollar will be the star and the euro an also-ran," said the European bank dealer. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0549 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw580139c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
