\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Welteke sees euro weakness correcting itself. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Steven Silber \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 2000\par 20:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT, May 7 (Reuters) - Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke played down the recent depreciation of the euro in an interview published on Sunday, saying it was being blown out of proportion by the media and would soon correct itself. \par \par \uc2  Welteke told Die Welt newspaper in the interview released ahead of publication on Monday that Europe had suffered similar and even greater bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility long before the euro was introduced. \par \par \uc2  He said the euro's current level was absolutely not in line with Europe's economic fundamentals, which were good. \par \par \uc2  Welteke, a member of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  council, declined to rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said it entailed risks because it invited speculators. \par \par \uc2  "The current external value of the euro is not at all justified by the fundamental data. I am convinced that it is an exaggeration of the markets which will soon correct itself," he told Die Welt. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are never ruled out. But they involve great risks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market invite speculators to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should therefore only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when it knows it will be successful," he added. \par \par \uc2  TIME TO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENE\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? \par \par \uc2  When asked if the time had come for the ECB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Welteke said: \par \par \uc2  "I will not tell you if the time has come or not. \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are ineffective as soon as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts to discuss them in public." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been awash with speculation that the ECB might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the relentless depreciation of the euro, which fell to a fresh all-time low of 88.40 U.S. cents last week. \par \par \uc2  But Welteke said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness was not a new phenomenon. \par \par \uc2  "In 1985 a dollar cost nearly 3.50 marks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. In 1995 we had an exchange rate of 1.36. The whole time prices in Germany were largely stable. It does irritate me that journalists don't measure the ECB by its mandate, but constantly focus on the exchange rate and thereby stoke panic." \par \par \uc2  Welteke also reiterated that euro zone growth was accelerating while U.S. growth was at risk of a slowdown. \par \par \uc2  In a fresh sign that the euro's weakness had been a clear factor in the ECB's decision to raise its main refinancing rate by a quarter point to 3.75 percent on April 27, Welteke said a half-point rise would have been worth considering had it been clear the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would react favourably. \par \par \uc2  "If we had known exactly that a rise by 50 basis points would lead to an appreciation of the euro, then such a rate move would have been worth considering. But the reaction of market participants may have turned out to be quite different." \par \par \uc2  Welteke also said coordination among the world's major economies - the United States, Europe and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - was a prerequisite for successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He added that Greece was too small to affect euro zone price stability upon its entry to the European Monetary Union (EMU). \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month Greece won the backing of the European Union's executive body to join the euro zone on January 1, 2001, despite concerns from the ECB about Greece's economic progress. ((Frankfurt Newsroom +49 69 756525, frankfurt.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw57011wq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
